window.IDX_BANK=[{"q":"How much clear working space do I need in front of a 120/240-volt residential-type panelboard?","k":["Dedicated equipment space","Working space","Clearances","Panelboards"],"ka":1,"s":["110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","408.18 Clearances","110.27 Guarding of Live Parts","240.24 Location in or on Premises"],"sa":0,"e":"Working space about equipment is indexed by that phrase, and it sends you to 110.26. There you find Table 110.26(A)(1) for depth (3 ft for most 150-volt-to-ground panels), 30 in. minimum width, and 6 1/2 ft headroom. Tip: when the question is about the room you need to stand in, think 'working space', not the equipment name.","r":"110.26(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The inspector asks whether our 6-hour heat-trace load counts as a 'continuous load'. Where is that term defined?","k":["Overcurrent protection","Continuous load","Heat tracing","Load, calculation of"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 100 Definitions","220.14 Other Loads - All Occupancies","210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","90.2 Scope"],"sa":0,"e":"Definitions in the NEC are indexed under the defined term itself, with a pointer to Art. 100. A continuous load is one where the maximum current is expected to continue for 3 hours or more. Tip: if the question asks 'what does this word mean', the keyword is that word and the page is Art. 100 (or the Part I definitions at the start of an article).","r":"Art. 100 (Continuous Load)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A new breaker is going in at the service. How do I find out if its interrupting rating is high enough?","k":["Short-circuit current","Circuit breakers","Interrupting rating","Overcurrent protection"],"ka":2,"s":["240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings","240.86 Series Ratings","110.3 Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing (Product Certification) of Equipment","110.9 Interrupting Rating"],"sa":3,"e":"'Interrupting rating' is a specific index entry that points straight to 110.9. It requires equipment to be rated for the current available at its line terminals. Tip: use the exact phrase from the nameplate or question; 'Circuit breakers' would give you pages of subentries.","r":"110.9","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What has to be posted on a panelboard in a commercial building that might be worked on while energized?","k":["Panelboards","Arc-flash hazard warning","Warning signs","Marking"],"ka":1,"s":["110.22 Identification of Disconnecting Means","408.4 Circuit Directory and Circuit Identification","110.21 Marking","110.16 Arc-Flash Hazard Warning"],"sa":3,"e":"The index lists 'Arc-flash hazard warning' and sends you to 110.16. It requires a field-applied label on switchboards, panelboards, industrial control panels, meter socket enclosures and motor control centers other than dwelling units. Tip: 'Marking' is too broad and would lead to 110.21 first.","r":"110.16(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Is a label showing available fault current required on a commercial service, and where do I look it up?","k":["Short-circuit current rating","Fault current, available","Service equipment","Labels"],"ka":1,"s":["110.10 Circuit Impedance, Short-Circuit Current Ratings, and Other Characteristics","110.24 Available Fault Current","230.82 Equipment Connected to the Supply Side of the Service Disconnect","110.9 Interrupting Rating"],"sa":1,"e":"Service equipment in other than dwelling units must be field marked with the maximum available fault current and the date it was calculated. The index entry 'Fault current, available' points to 110.24. Tip: look for the electrical term, not the equipment type.","r":"110.24(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"On a 120/240-volt 4-wire delta system, how must the 208-volt-to-ground conductor be identified?","k":["Delta systems","High leg","Phase marking","Identification of conductors"],"ka":1,"s":["200.6 Means of Identifying Grounded Conductors","250.119 Identification of Equipment Grounding Conductors","110.15 High-Leg Marking","210.5 Identification for Branch Circuits"],"sa":2,"e":"The high-leg is marked orange or another effective means wherever a neutral is present. It is a named index entry that points to 110.15. Tip: the 208 V leg is a classic exam trap; remember 'orange, and B phase in the middle' for the panel layout.","r":"110.15","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where do I find the rule on labeling what each disconnect switch controls?","k":["Switches","Labels","Identification","Disconnecting means"],"ka":3,"s":["225.37 Identification","110.22 Identification of Disconnecting Means","404.8 Accessibility and Grouping","230.70 General"],"sa":1,"e":"110.22 requires each disconnecting means to be legibly marked to indicate its purpose, in a durable manner that suits the environment. The index entry 'Disconnecting means' contains a sub-entry for identification. Tip: with Disconnecting means, scan the subentries for 'identification' before turning pages.","r":"110.22(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A newly installed panelboard's circuits must be identified. Where is the directory requirement?","k":["Labels","Panelboards","Circuit directory","Branch circuits"],"ka":1,"s":["210.5 Identification for Branch Circuits","408.3 Support and Arrangement of Busbars and Conductors","110.22 Identification of Disconnecting Means","408.4 Circuit Directory and Circuit Identification"],"sa":3,"e":"Every circuit and circuit modification must be legibly identified as to purpose in a directory at the panel, with enough detail to be distinguished from all others; spare positions are listed too. The index lists 'Panelboards' with a sub-entry for circuit directory. Tip: 210.5 is about conductor color; 408.4 is about the directory.","r":"408.4(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How many kitchen small-appliance branch circuits does a dwelling unit need?","k":["Receptacle outlets","Kitchens","Dwelling units, branch circuits","Small-appliance branch circuits"],"ka":3,"s":["210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","220.52 Small-Appliance and Laundry Loads - Dwelling Unit"],"sa":2,"e":"210.11(C)(1) requires two or more 20-ampere small-appliance branch circuits for the kitchen, pantry, dining room and similar areas. The index lists 'Small-appliance branch circuits' directly. Tip: 210.52 tells where receptacles go; 210.11 tells how many circuits; 220.52 handles the load calculation.","r":"210.11(C)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where must receptacles be placed on a kitchen countertop wall space?","k":["Countertops","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit","Small-appliance branch circuits","Kitchens"],"ka":1,"s":["210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","406.4 General Installation Requirements"],"sa":0,"e":"Receptacle outlets for dwellings are located by 210.52, and 210.52(C) covers countertops: no point along the wall line more than 24 in. from a receptacle, and every wall space 12 in. or wider gets one. Tip: 'Receptacles' alone is too generic; use the 'dwelling unit' subentry.","r":"210.52(C)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A hallway in a house is 12 ft long. Does it need a receptacle?","k":["Wall space","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit","Hallways","Habitable rooms"],"ka":1,"s":["210.70 Lighting Outlets Required","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","210.11 Branch Circuits Required"],"sa":1,"e":"210.52(H) requires at least one receptacle in hallways 10 ft or more in length, measured along the centerline. The rule sits in the dwelling unit receptacle section. Tip: memorize the lettered list: kitchen (C), bath (D), outdoor (E), laundry (F), basements/garages (G), hallways (H), foyers (I).","r":"210.52(H)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which dwelling locations require GFCI protection for 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles?","k":["Wet locations","Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Personnel protection"],"ka":2,"s":["406.4 General Installation Requirements","590.6 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel","210.12 Arc-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel"],"sa":3,"e":"The index entry 'Ground-fault circuit interrupters' lists protection for personnel at 210.8. 210.8(A) includes bathrooms, garages, outdoors, crawl spaces, unfinished basements, kitchens, laundry areas and areas near sinks, among others. Tip: GFCI is shock protection (210.8); AFCI is fire protection (210.12).","r":"210.8(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which rooms in a house must have arc-fault protection on the branch circuits?","k":["Branch circuits","Bedrooms","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Arc-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":3,"s":["210.12 Arc-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","406.12 Tamper-Resistant Receptacles","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel"],"sa":0,"e":"AFCI is indexed under 'Arc-fault circuit interrupters', which points to 210.12. 210.12(A) lists kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, parlors, libraries, dens, bedrooms, sunrooms, recreation rooms, closets, hallways, laundry areas and similar rooms or areas. Tip: GFCI sits right before AFCI in the index, so read the full phrase.","r":"210.12(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Do I need tamper-resistant receptacles in a new dwelling bedroom?","k":["Bedrooms","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit","Receptacles, tamper-resistant","Child safety"],"ka":2,"s":["406.4 General Installation Requirements","406.12 Tamper-Resistant Receptacles","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations"],"sa":1,"e":"406.12 requires listed tamper-resistant receptacles of 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere ratings in dwelling units and in other occupancies it lists, such as guest rooms and child care facilities. The index lists it under Receptacles. Tip: the article for receptacle devices is 406, while where to place them is 210.52.","r":"406.12","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What outdoor receptacle outlets does a one-family house require?","k":["Outdoor receptacles","Weatherproof","Balconies","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit"],"ka":3,"s":["225.18 Clearance for Overhead Conductors and Cables","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations"],"sa":2,"e":"210.52(E) requires at least one receptacle at the front and one at the back, readily accessible from grade and not more than 6 1/2 ft above grade; balconies, decks and porches also need one. Tip: finding the lettered subsection is faster than reading the whole section.","r":"210.52(E)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where do I find the rule for the receptacle outlet that must be installed near rooftop or attic HVAC equipment?","k":["Air-conditioning","Heating, air-conditioning, and refrigeration equipment outlet","Receptacle outlets","Service receptacle"],"ka":1,"s":["440.14 Location","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.64 Electrical Service Areas","210.63 Heating, Air-Conditioning, and Refrigeration Equipment Outlet"],"sa":3,"e":"A 125-volt, single-phase, 15- or 20-ampere receptacle must be installed at an accessible location for servicing, on the same level and within 25 ft of the equipment, and not on the equipment's load side of the disconnect. Tip: when the index entry reads like the section title, use it.","r":"210.63","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which lights must be wall-switch controlled in a house?","k":["Habitable rooms","Luminaires","Switches","Lighting outlets"],"ka":3,"s":["410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.70 Lighting Outlets Required","404.2 Switch Connections"],"sa":2,"e":"210.70(A) requires at least one wall-switch-controlled lighting outlet in every habitable room and bathroom, plus hallways, stairways, attached garages and outdoor entrances; occupancy sensors are permitted in some cases. Tip: 'Lighting outlets' is the index entry that is also the section's subject.","r":"210.70(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Two circuits share a neutral. How must they be disconnected?","k":["Branch circuits","Neutral conductors","Multiwire branch circuits","Shared neutrals"],"ka":2,"s":["210.7 Multiple Branch Circuits","210.4 Multiwire Branch Circuits","220.61 Feeder or Service Neutral Load","200.4 Neutral Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"A multiwire branch circuit is a named index entry, and 210.4(B) requires a means to disconnect simultaneously all ungrounded conductors at the point where the branch circuit originates, such as handle ties or a common-trip breaker. Tip: 210.7 is for multiple branch circuits on one yoke; 210.4 is the multiwire concept.","r":"210.4(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the proper color for a branch-circuit grounded (neutral) conductor?","k":["White wire","Color code","Grounded conductors, identification","Neutral conductors"],"ka":2,"s":["210.5 Identification for Branch Circuits","310.120 Marking","200.6 Means of Identifying Grounded Conductors","250.119 Identification of Equipment Grounding Conductors"],"sa":2,"e":"The grounded conductor is identified white, gray, or by three continuous white stripes on other than green insulation (200.6(A)). The NEC groups grounded conductor color rules in Art. 200. Tip: 250.119 is for the green equipment grounding conductor; 210.5 is for ungrounded conductors.","r":"200.6(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 20-amp breaker feeds a load that runs for 5 hours at a time. What does the Code say about the size of the overcurrent device?","k":["Branch circuits, rating","Overcurrent protection","Ampacity","Continuous loads"],"ka":3,"s":["210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","210.20 Overcurrent Protection","220.14 Other Loads - All Occupancies","240.4 Protection of Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"The overcurrent device for a branch circuit with continuous loads must be rated at least the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load (210.20(A)). 'Continuous loads' in the index lists the sections for conductors and devices. Tip: 210.19 sizes the wire at 125 percent; 210.20 sizes the breaker.","r":"210.20(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How many service disconnects are permitted for one building?","k":["Main breaker","Number of services","Service disconnecting means","Service equipment"],"ka":2,"s":["230.2 Number of Services","230.70 General","230.79 Rating of Service Disconnecting Means","230.71 Maximum Number of Disconnects"],"sa":3,"e":"The service disconnecting means for each service may consist of not more than six switches or six circuit breakers in a single enclosure or group of separate enclosures. 230.71 is the rule for the 'six-handle rule'. Tip: do not confuse it with 230.2, which concerns the number of services.","r":"230.71","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the minimum rating of the service disconnect for a one-family dwelling?","k":["Service size","Service disconnecting means","Main breaker","Dwelling units, services"],"ka":1,"s":["230.79 Rating of Service Disconnecting Means","230.42 Minimum Size and Rating","230.71 Maximum Number of Disconnects","310.12 Single-Phase Dwelling Services and Feeders"],"sa":0,"e":"230.79(C) says a one-family dwelling service disconnect is at least 100 amperes, 3-wire. Look under 'Service disconnecting means' and find the rating subentry. Tip: 230.42 sizes the service conductors; 230.79 sizes the disconnect.","r":"230.79(C)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A house gets a new service. Is an outdoor emergency disconnect required?","k":["Emergency disconnects","Service disconnecting means","Firefighter shutoff","Service equipment"],"ka":0,"s":["230.70 General","230.72 Grouping of Disconnects","230.85 Emergency Disconnects","225.31 Disconnecting Means"],"sa":2,"e":"230.85 requires a readily accessible outdoor emergency disconnect for one- and two-family dwelling services, marked as such. It is indexed as 'Emergency disconnects'. Tip: 'Emergency' also leads to Art. 700; the qualifier 'disconnects' places you in Art. 230.","r":"230.85","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Is surge protection required at a new dwelling service?","k":["Surge protective devices","Service equipment","Surge arresters","Lightning protection"],"ka":0,"s":["230.67 Surge Protection","Art. 280 Surge Arresters, Over 1000 Volts","230.82 Equipment Connected to the Supply Side of the Service Disconnect","Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection"],"sa":0,"e":"230.67 requires a listed Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at services supplying dwelling units, installed in or adjacent to the service equipment. It is indexed under 'Surge protective devices'. Tip: Art. 242 gives the general SPD rules; Art. 230 tells you when one is required at the service.","r":"230.67","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How high does a service drop have to be over a residential driveway?","k":["Driveways","Overhead service","Clearances","Service-drop conductors"],"ka":3,"s":["230.26 Point of Attachment","225.18 Clearance for Overhead Conductors and Cables","230.24 Clearances","230.9 Clearance from Building Openings"],"sa":2,"e":"230.24(B) gives vertical clearances for service-drop conductors over areas such as driveways and roads. The index lists clearances under 'Service-drop conductors'. Tip: 225.18 is the same idea for outside branch circuits and feeders, so check whether the conductors are service conductors first.","r":"230.24(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What standard ampere ratings of fuses and breakers does the Code list?","k":["Circuit breakers","Fuses","Ampere ratings, standard","Overcurrent protection"],"ka":2,"s":["240.4 Protection of Conductors","240.21 Location in Circuit","240.2 Definitions","240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings"],"sa":3,"e":"240.6(A) lists the standard ampere ratings for fuses and inverse time circuit breakers, such as 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90, 100, 110, 125, 150, 175, 200, 225, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 700, 800, 1000, 1200 and higher values. Tip: 'standard' is the key word.","r":"240.6(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A feeder of 1 AWG copper (130 amps at 75 degrees C) does not match a standard breaker size. May I protect it with the next size up?","k":["Conductors, protection","Feeders","Overcurrent protection","Ampacity"],"ka":2,"s":["240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings","240.4 Protection of Conductors","215.3 Overcurrent Protection","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts"],"sa":1,"e":"240.4(B) allows the next higher standard rating if the conductors do not supply multioutlet receptacle branch circuits, the ampacity does not match a standard rating, and the rating is 800 amps or less. 'Overcurrent protection' in the index points to Art. 240 and its sections. Tip: 240.4(B) is the 'next size up' rule.","r":"240.4(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What are the maximum overcurrent device ratings for 14, 12 and 10 AWG copper?","k":["Overcurrent protection","Small conductors","Branch circuits","Ampacity"],"ka":0,"s":["210.20 Overcurrent Protection","240.4 Protection of Conductors","240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings","310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"240.4(D) limits 14 AWG copper to 15 A, 12 AWG copper to 20 A and 10 AWG copper to 30 A after any adjustment, with the exception of motor and some other circuits. Tip: these are called 'small conductor' rules; look for them within the protection of conductors section.","r":"240.4(D)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can a panelboard be mounted in a bathroom or a clothes closet of a house?","k":["Bathrooms","Overcurrent protection","Clothes closets","Panelboards"],"ka":1,"s":["408.37 Panelboards in Damp or Wet Locations","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","240.21 Location in Circuit","240.24 Location in or on Premises"],"sa":3,"e":"240.24(E) prohibits overcurrent devices in bathrooms of dwelling units and guest rooms, and 240.24(D) prohibits them in the vicinity of easily ignitable material such as clothes closets. Find it under 'Overcurrent protection, location'. Tip: 110.26 spacing rules also apply, but the 'where' rule is in 240.24.","r":"240.24(D) and (E)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How long can a feeder tap be without overcurrent protection at the tap?","k":["Taps","Feeders","Conductors, tap","Overcurrent protection"],"ka":0,"s":["210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","215.3 Overcurrent Protection","240.21 Location in Circuit","240.4 Protection of Conductors"],"sa":2,"e":"Tap rules such as the 10 ft and 25 ft feeder tap rules are in 240.21(B). 'Taps' is a direct index entry. Tip: 240.21 is about where the overcurrent device must be located in the circuit.","r":"240.21(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where is the requirement for arc energy reduction on a large breaker found?","k":["Arc energy reduction","Circuit breakers","Maintenance mode","Arc-flash hazard warning"],"ka":0,"s":["240.86 Series Ratings","110.16 Arc-Flash Hazard Warning","240.12 Electrical System Coordination","240.87 Arc Energy Reduction"],"sa":3,"e":"240.87 applies where the highest continuous current trip setting of the circuit breaker is 1200 amperes or higher, and offers methods such as zone-selective interlocking, differential relaying or an energy-reducing maintenance switch. Tip: 110.16 is the label; 240.87 is the engineering requirement.","r":"240.87","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What counts as a grounding electrode, and what is allowed?","k":["Water pipe","Ground rods","Grounding electrode system","Grounding electrodes"],"ka":3,"s":["250.50 Grounding Electrode System","250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor","250.53 Grounding Electrode System Installation","250.52 Grounding Electrodes"],"sa":3,"e":"250.52(A) lists metal underground water pipe, metal in-ground support structure, concrete-encased electrodes (Ufer), ground rings, rod and pipe electrodes, plate electrodes and other local metal underground systems. 250.50 says to use all that are present. Tip: 250.52 lists them, 250.53 tells you how to install them.","r":"250.52(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the length and installation rule for a driven ground rod?","k":["Grounding electrodes","Ground rods","Grounding electrode conductor","Rod electrodes"],"ka":1,"s":["250.53 Grounding Electrode System Installation","250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor","250.64 Grounding Electrode Conductor Installation","250.52 Grounding Electrodes"],"sa":0,"e":"250.52(A)(5) says rod electrodes must be at least 8 ft in length, and 250.53(A) covers installation, including driven to full length, spacing and the single-rod supplement rule unless the rod proves 25 ohms or less. Tip: the index points to both; choose 250.53 when the question is about installation.","r":"250.53(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How big must the grounding electrode conductor be for a 200-amp service with 3/0 copper conductors?","k":["Grounding electrode conductor","Service-entrance conductors","Ground rods","Grounding electrode system"],"ka":0,"s":["250.64 Grounding Electrode Conductor Installation","250.122 Size of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems","250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor"],"sa":3,"e":"Table 250.66 sizes the GEC from the largest service-entrance conductor (or equivalent area for parallel sets). For 3/0 copper (2/0 to 3/0 range) the GEC is 4 AWG copper. If it goes to a rod only, 250.66(A) caps it at 6 AWG. Tip: the table name is in the section title.","r":"Table 250.66","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What size equipment grounding conductor goes with a 60-amp circuit breaker?","k":["Grounding conductors","Overcurrent protection","Equipment grounding conductors","Bonding jumpers"],"ka":2,"s":["250.118 Types of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.122 Size of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.102 Grounded Conductor, Bonding Conductors, and Jumpers","250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 250.122 sizes the EGC from the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of the circuit; 60 A requires 10 AWG copper. Tip: GEC is sized from the service conductors (250.66); EGC is sized from the breaker or fuse (250.122).","r":"Table 250.122","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Does the gas piping in a house have to be bonded, and how?","k":["Equipment grounding conductors","Bonding","Gas piping","Piping systems"],"ka":1,"s":["250.118 Types of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.104 Bonding of Piping Systems and Exposed Structural Metal","250.94 Bonding for Other Systems","250.102 Grounded Conductor, Bonding Conductors, and Jumpers"],"sa":1,"e":"Metal piping systems that may become energized, such as gas piping, are bonded under 250.104(B) to the equipment grounding conductor of the circuit that is likely to energize the piping. Tip: water piping is bonded under 250.104(A); both are in the same section.","r":"250.104(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where do I tie the cable and telephone grounds together at a house?","k":["Communications circuits","Intersystem bonding termination","Grounding electrode system","Bonding"],"ka":1,"s":["250.64 Grounding Electrode Conductor Installation","250.94 Bonding for Other Systems","250.50 Grounding Electrode System","820.100 Cable Bonding and Grounding"],"sa":1,"e":"250.94 requires an intersystem bonding termination at the service or at the metering equipment enclosure, so communications and CATV systems can bond to the grounding electrode system. Tip: Chapter 8 gives the communications side; Art. 250 gives the termination device.","r":"250.94","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How do I ground a new subpanel in a detached garage fed by a feeder?","k":["Buildings or structures supplied by feeder","Subpanels","Separate buildings","Grounding electrode conductor"],"ka":0,"s":["225.30 Number of Supplies","250.32 Buildings or Structures Supplied by Feeder(s) or Branch Circuit(s)","250.30 Grounding Separately Derived Alternating-Current Systems","250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems"],"sa":1,"e":"250.32 requires a grounding electrode system at the separate building, a 4-wire feeder with the neutral not bonded to the enclosure, and an equipment grounding conductor run with the feeder. Tip: neutrals are bonded to ground only at the service or a separately derived system.","r":"250.32(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A dry-type transformer makes a new 208Y/120 panel. Where is the grounding of a separately derived system covered?","k":["Grounding electrode system","Transformers","Separately derived systems","Bonding"],"ka":2,"s":["250.28 Main Bonding Jumper and System Bonding Jumper","450.10 Grounding","250.30 Grounding Separately Derived Alternating-Current Systems","250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems"],"sa":2,"e":"250.30 covers bonding jumper, supply-side bonding jumper, grounding electrode conductor and grounding electrode for derived systems such as transformers and generators. The index lists 'Separately derived systems' as a main entry. Tip: Art. 100 defines the term; Art. 250 tells you how to ground it.","r":"250.30","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May an EMT conduit serve as the equipment grounding conductor?","k":["Conduit as ground","Equipment grounding conductors","Raceways, grounding","Electrical metallic tubing"],"ka":1,"s":["250.122 Size of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.118 Types of Equipment Grounding Conductors","250.4 General Requirements for Grounding and Bonding","358.60 Grounding"],"sa":1,"e":"250.118 lists the permitted types, including EMT, RMC, IMC and listed fittings. Look in the index under 'Equipment grounding conductors' for 'types'. Tip: 358.60 also allows EMT as an EGC, so either may be cited.","r":"250.118(4)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May a new 240-volt clothes dryer receptacle use a 3-wire cord and receptacle?","k":["Frames, grounding","Branch circuits, appliance","Clothes dryers","Ranges"],"ka":2,"s":["250.138 Cord-and-Plug-Connected Equipment","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating","250.134 Equipment Fastened in Place or Connected by Permanent Wiring Methods (Fixed) - Grounding","250.140 Frames of Ranges and Clothes Dryers"],"sa":3,"e":"250.140 says frames of ranges and dryers must be grounded per 250.134 using the equipment grounding conductor, which means a 4-wire circuit for new installations. The old 3-wire rule is gone for new work. Tip: look under 'Clothes dryers' or 'Ranges' for the grounding sub-entry.","r":"250.140","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What unit load in volt-amperes per square foot do I use for the general lighting of a warehouse?","k":["Lighting load","Warehouses","Load calculations","Square footage"],"ka":0,"s":["220.42 General Lighting","220.14 Other Loads - All Occupancies","220.12 Lighting Load for Non-Dwelling Occupancies","220.52 Small-Appliance and Laundry Loads - Dwelling Unit"],"sa":2,"e":"220.12 sends you to Table 220.12, which lists the general lighting unit load by occupancy; warehouses (storage) are 1/4 VA per sq ft. The index entry 'Lighting load' points to the table in Art. 220 Part II. Tip: demand factors for the same lighting load are in Part III (220.42), a separate step.","r":"220.12 and Table 220.12","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the demand factor for a household electric range?","k":["Ranges","Dwelling unit load","Cooking equipment, demand factors","Appliance loads"],"ka":2,"s":["220.61 Feeder or Service Neutral Load","220.55 Electric Cooking Appliances in Dwelling Units and Household Cooking Appliances Used in Instructional Programs","220.53 Appliance Load - Dwelling Unit(s)","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 220.55 gives demand factors for household ranges, wall-mounted ovens and counter-mounted cooking units; Column C gives the 8 kW rating for a single range not over 12 kW. The index lists cooking equipment demand factors under Art. 220. Tip: a 12 kW range counts as 8 kW for the feeder calculation.","r":"220.55 and Table 220.55","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How do I figure the calculated load for a whole house using the optional method?","k":["Service size","Demand factors","Optional calculation","Load calculations, dwelling units"],"ka":3,"s":["220.83 Existing Dwelling Unit","220.82 Dwelling Unit - Optional Calculation","220.40 General","220.52 Small-Appliance and Laundry Loads - Dwelling Unit"],"sa":1,"e":"The optional calculation takes all loads at 100 percent for the first 10 kVA and 40 percent of the remainder, plus heating and air-conditioning at the percentages in 220.82(C). It is for dwellings with a single 120/240-volt, 3-wire service or feeder of 100 amps or more. Tip: 220.83 is the version for adding loads to an existing dwelling.","r":"220.82","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"On a feeder neutral, can I apply a 70 percent demand to the part of the neutral load above 200 amps?","k":["Grounded conductor","Neutral conductors, load","Feeders","Demand factors"],"ka":1,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","200.4 Neutral Conductors","220.61 Feeder or Service Neutral Load"],"sa":3,"e":"The feeder or service neutral is the maximum unbalanced load, and 220.61(B) permits a 70 percent demand on the portion over 200 amps. Nonlinear loads limit this reduction. Tip: it is a load rule, so it lives in Art. 220, not Art. 310.","r":"220.61","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the minimum size of feeder conductors serving a continuous load plus noncontinuous load?","k":["Continuous loads","Feeders","Ampacity","Conductors, size"],"ka":1,"s":["210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","215.3 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":1,"e":"Feeders are indexed under 'Feeders', and 215.2(A)(1) requires an ampacity of at least the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load. 210.19 is the same rule for branch circuits. Tip: Art. 215 is feeders, Art. 210 is branch circuits.","r":"215.2(A)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How far above the ground must outdoor overhead feeder conductors be at a residential driveway?","k":["Clearances","Feeders, outside","Driveways","Overhead conductors, clearances"],"ka":3,"s":["225.19 Clearances from Buildings for Conductors of Not over 1000 Volts, Nominal","225.6 Conductor Size and Support","225.18 Clearance for Overhead Conductors and Cables","230.24 Clearances"],"sa":2,"e":"225.18 lists vertical clearances for overhead conductors and cables of not over 1000 volts, such as 10 ft over residential property and driveways in specific cases, 12 ft, 15 ft and 18 ft over public streets and roads. Tip: 230.24 applies to service-drop conductors, 225.18 to all other outside branch circuits and feeders.","r":"225.18","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A detached shop is fed by a feeder from the house. Where must the disconnect be?","k":["Detached buildings","Outside branch circuits and feeders","Disconnecting means","Feeders"],"ka":1,"s":["225.32 Location","225.30 Number of Supplies","225.31 Disconnecting Means","230.70 General"],"sa":0,"e":"225.31 says a disconnect is required, and 225.32 says where it goes: either outside or inside nearest the point of entrance of the feeder conductors, readily accessible. The index entry 'Outside branch circuits and feeders' (Art. 225) leads you to the location section. Tip: 'Location' is one of the standard section titles in many Article 225 and 230 Parts.","r":"225.32","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which ampacity table applies for 4 AWG THHN copper in conduit at 30 C ambient?","k":["Allowable ampacity tables","Conductors, insulated","Terminals","Ampacities of conductors"],"ka":3,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","Table 310.15(C)(1) Adjustment Factors for More Than Three Current-Carrying Conductors in a Raceway or Cable","Table 400.5(A)(1) Allowable Ampacity for Flexible Cords","Table 310.104(A) Conductor Applications and Insulations Rated 600 Volts"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 310.16 has three temperature columns: 60, 75 and 90 degrees C. For 4 AWG copper THHN the 90 C column gives 95 amps, but the circuit is limited by terminal ratings (usually the 75 C column, 85 amps) under 110.14(C). Tip: the correction and adjustment tables in 310.15 are separate from 310.16.","r":"Table 310.16","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"I have six current-carrying conductors in one raceway. What derating applies?","k":["Ambient temperature","Raceways, number of conductors","Ampacities of conductors, adjustment factors","Conduit fill"],"ka":2,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","Table 310.15(C)(1) Adjustment Factors for More Than Three Current-Carrying Conductors in a Raceway or Cable","300.17 Number and Size of Conductors in Raceway","358.22 Number of Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 310.15(C)(1) requires an adjustment factor when more than three current-carrying conductors are in a raceway or cable: 80 percent for 4 to 6 conductors, 70 percent for 7 to 9, 50 percent for 10 to 20. Tip: this is adjustment (number of wires); correction (310.15(B)) is for temperature.","r":"310.15(C)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What temperature rating and permitted use does THWN-2 insulation have?","k":["Wire types","Conductors, insulated","Insulation, conductor","Temperature ratings"],"ka":2,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","310.10 Uses Permitted","310.104 Conductor Constructions and Applications","310.120 Marking"],"sa":2,"e":"Table 310.104(A) in 310.104 lists each insulation type (THHN, THWN-2, XHHW-2 and so on) with its maximum operating temperature and the permitted application, such as 90 C dry and wet locations for THWN-2. Tip: the type letters decode there, while 310.16 only gives ampacity by temperature column.","r":"Table 310.104(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A dwelling has a 200-amp service with 4/0 aluminum. Is that allowed?","k":["Service size","Service-entrance conductors","Dwelling units, service conductors","Aluminum conductors"],"ka":2,"s":["215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","310.12 Single-Phase Dwelling Services and Feeders","230.42 Minimum Size and Rating","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts"],"sa":1,"e":"310.12 permits 120/240-volt, single-phase dwelling service and feeder conductors to be sized at 83 percent of the service or feeder rating; 4/0 aluminum is listed for 200 A. Look up dwelling service conductors. Tip: this rule was in 310.15(B)(7) before the 2023 NEC.","r":"310.12(C)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How many 12 AWG conductors can fit in a 3 x 2 x 3 1/2 in. device box?","k":["Box fill","Boxes, number of conductors","Conductors in boxes","Device boxes"],"ka":1,"s":["314.28 Pull and Junction Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures","314.27 Outlet Boxes","314.24 Depth of Outlet Boxes","314.16 Number of Conductors in Outlet, Device, and Junction Boxes, and Conduit Bodies"],"sa":3,"e":"314.16 uses Table 314.16(A), which lists an 18.0 cu in. capacity for that device box. Each 12 AWG conductor needs 2.25 cu in. per Table 314.16(B), a duplex receptacle counts as two allowances, and clamps and grounds add their own, so 18.0 cu in. holds 8 allowances in total. Tip: work the count: volume allowance per conductor, then compare.","r":"314.16(A) and (B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How many cubic inches does a 12 AWG conductor take up for box fill?","k":["Volume allowance","Conductor fill","Box fill","Boxes, number of conductors"],"ka":2,"s":["314.16 Number of Conductors in Outlet, Device, and Junction Boxes, and Conduit Bodies","310.16 Allowable Ampacities","314.24 Depth of Outlet Boxes","Chapter 9 Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 314.16(B) gives 2.25 cu in. for 12 AWG, 2.0 for 14 AWG, 2.5 for 10 AWG, 3.0 for 8 AWG and 5.0 for 6 AWG. Tip: do not use Chapter 9 conductor-area tables for box fill; boxes use the volume allowance table.","r":"Table 314.16(B)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A box holds two cables with clamps, one duplex receptacle and ground wires. How do I count the clamp and device allowances?","k":["Equipment grounding conductors","Receptacles","Cable clamps","Box fill"],"ka":3,"s":["314.17 Conductors That Enter Boxes, Conduit Bodies, or Fittings","406.4 General Installation Requirements","314.27 Outlet Boxes","314.16 Number of Conductors in Outlet, Device, and Junction Boxes, and Conduit Bodies"],"sa":3,"e":"314.16(B) counts one allowance (largest conductor) for all internal clamps, two allowances for each yoke or strap (device), one allowance for all equipment grounding conductors, and so on. Tip: 'Box fill' is the term inspectors use, and the index has it.","r":"314.16(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How big must a junction box be for a 2-in. conduit straight pull?","k":["Conduit bodies","Pull boxes","Boxes, sizing","Junction boxes"],"ka":1,"s":["314.29 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures to Be Accessible","314.28 Pull and Junction Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures","314.16 Number of Conductors in Outlet, Device, and Junction Boxes, and Conduit Bodies","300.15 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, or Fittings - Where Required"],"sa":1,"e":"For raceways containing conductors of 4 AWG or larger, a straight pull box length must be at least 8 times the trade diameter of the largest raceway (314.28(A)(1)). For a 2-in. conduit that is 16 in. Angle pulls use 6 times the largest raceway plus the sum of the others. Tip: 'Pull boxes' is the first-line index term.","r":"314.28(A)(1)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can I leave a junction box covered by drywall?","k":["Junction boxes","Boxes, accessible","Covers","Concealed wiring"],"ka":1,"s":["300.15 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, or Fittings - Where Required","314.20 Flush-Mounted Installations","314.25 Covers and Canopies","314.29 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures to Be Accessible"],"sa":3,"e":"314.29 requires boxes and conduit bodies to be installed so the wiring contained in them can be rendered accessible without removing any part of the building. Tip: 'accessible' is its own search word; not the same as 'readily accessible' in Art. 100.","r":"314.29","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A plaster-ring box is set into a plaster wall with a gap. How close must it be to the surface?","k":["Gaps around boxes","Boxes, flush-mounted installations","Noncombustible surfaces","Plaster rings"],"ka":1,"s":["314.21 Repairing Noncombustible Surfaces","314.20 Flush-Mounted Installations","314.25 Covers and Canopies","314.23 Supports"],"sa":1,"e":"In walls of noncombustible material, boxes must be set so the front edge is not more than 1/4 in. back of the finished surface; in combustible walls the box must be flush or project. Tip: the gap rules for plaster are in 314.21.","r":"314.20","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How far from an NM cable's entrance to a box must I staple it?","k":["Staples","Cable supports","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Boxes"],"ka":2,"s":["334.30 Securing and Supporting","300.11 Securing and Supporting","334.24 Bending Radius","334.15 Exposed Work"],"sa":0,"e":"Type NM cable must be secured within 12 in. of every box and at intervals not exceeding 4 1/2 ft. Short runs exceptions exist for fished cable and single-gang boxes with cable clamps within 8 in. Tip: use the article index, then the 'Securing and Supporting' section, which has the same title in every cable article.","r":"334.30","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How tight can I bend Romex around a corner?","k":["Bending radius","Cable bends","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Conductors, bending"],"ka":2,"s":["300.34 Conductor Bending Radius","334.30 Securing and Supporting","334.24 Bending Radius","312.6 Deflection of Conductors"],"sa":2,"e":"Bends in Type NM cable shall be made so the cable is not damaged, and the radius of the curve of the inner edge must be not less than 5 times the diameter of the cable. Tip: 300.34 gives conductor bending radius for the over-1000-volt side. Use the cable article's bending-radius section.","r":"334.24","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May NM cable be run on the surface of a basement wall?","k":["Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Basements","Cable protection","Exposed work"],"ka":0,"s":["334.15 Exposed Work","300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage","334.10 Uses Permitted","334.12 Uses Not Permitted"],"sa":0,"e":"334.15 says exposed NM cable must closely follow the surface of the building finish or running boards, and be protected from physical damage where subject to it (for example with conduit, EMT, guard strips). Tip: 334.12 lists the places NM cannot go, such as commercial garages, hoistways.","r":"334.15(A) and (B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How many quarter bends can I put in an EMT run between pull points?","k":["Conduit, bends","Pull points","Bends","Electrical metallic tubing"],"ka":3,"s":["358.26 Bends - Number in One Run","358.30 Securing and Supporting","358.24 Bends - How Made","358.22 Number of Conductors"],"sa":0,"e":"There shall be no more than the equivalent of four quarter bends (360 degrees total) between pull points, such as conduit bodies and boxes. The raceway articles all have a parallel section. Tip: for RMC it is 344.26, for PVC 352.26.","r":"358.26","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How often must EMT be supported?","k":["Electrical metallic tubing","Conduit supports","Straps","Raceway supports"],"ka":0,"s":["358.10 Uses Permitted","358.30 Securing and Supporting","300.19 Supporting Conductors in Vertical Raceways","300.11 Securing and Supporting"],"sa":1,"e":"EMT must be secured at least every 10 ft and within 3 ft of each outlet box, junction box, device box, cabinet, conduit body or other tubing termination. Tip: use the raceway name first; 300.11 is the general rule for support of raceways in ceilings.","r":"358.30(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the support interval for PVC conduit in a 2-in. run?","k":["Nonmetallic raceways","Conduit supports","Rigid polyvinyl chloride conduit","PVC"],"ka":2,"s":["352.44 Expansion Fittings","352.30 Securing and Supporting","352.26 Bends - Number in One Run","300.11 Securing and Supporting"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 352.30 lists maximum spacing by trade size; 2-in. PVC needs support at least every 5 ft, and 1/2- to 1-in. every 3 ft. Support is also required within 3 ft of each box or termination. Tip: use the 'Securing and Supporting' section within the article for that raceway.","r":"Table 352.30","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How far must I bury PVC conduit under a residential driveway?","k":["PVC","Underground installations","Trenching","Direct burial"],"ka":1,"s":["300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage","352.10 Uses Permitted","300.5 Underground Installations","300.50 Underground Installations"],"sa":2,"e":"Table 300.5 gives the minimum cover: 18 in. for PVC under a residential driveway or other locations, 24 in. under streets and parking lots, and 6 in. for rigid metal conduit. For 120-volt, 20-amp or less GFCI-protected residential circuits, 12 in. applies. Tip: 300.50 is the same subject for over 1000 volts.","r":"Table 300.5","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The cable runs through studs near the face. How far must it be from the edge?","k":["Nail plates","Cable protection","Physical damage, protection against","Studs"],"ka":2,"s":["300.15 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, or Fittings - Where Required","300.11 Securing and Supporting","300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage","334.15 Exposed Work"],"sa":2,"e":"300.4(A)(1) requires holes drilled at least 1 1/4 in. from the nearest edge of the wood member, or protection with a steel plate or sleeve at least 1/16 in. thick. Cables running parallel to framing follow 300.4(D). Tip: the index entry names the problem, not the wiring method.","r":"300.4(A) and (D)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May I support EMT from the ceiling grid wires of a suspended ceiling?","k":["Ceiling grid","Securing and supporting of raceways","Raceway supports","Suspended ceilings"],"ka":1,"s":["300.12 Mechanical Continuity - Raceways and Cables","300.11 Securing and Supporting","358.30 Securing and Supporting","300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage"],"sa":1,"e":"300.11(A) says raceways and cables must be securely fastened in place and not supported by ceiling grid support wires unless identified for the purpose; independent support wires are needed. Tip: 'Securing and supporting' is a generic term of art; the article-specific section sits below.","r":"300.11(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can all conductors of a circuit be run in separate conduits?","k":["Raceways","Conductors, in same raceway","Parallel conductors","Induced currents"],"ka":1,"s":["300.20 Induced Currents in Ferrous Metal Enclosures or Ferrous Metal Raceways","300.3 Conductors","300.5 Underground Installations","310.10 Uses Permitted"],"sa":1,"e":"300.3(B) requires all conductors of the same circuit, and the grounded conductor and all equipment grounding conductors, to be contained within the same raceway, cable, trench or cord. Separating them leads to induced currents in ferrous metal. Tip: parallel sets are covered in 310.10(G).","r":"300.3(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A conduit passes through a 2-hour fire-rated wall. What must be done to the opening around it?","k":["Raceways, fire barriers","Penetrations","Fire spread, wiring","Firestopping"],"ka":2,"s":["110.12 Mechanical Execution of Work","300.22 Wiring in Ducts Not Used for Air Handling, Fabricated Ducts, and Other Spaces for Environmental Air (Plenums)","300.21 Spread of Fire or Products of Combustion","300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage"],"sa":2,"e":"Penetrations of fire-resistance-rated walls, floors and ceilings must be firestopped using approved methods to maintain the rating (300.21). Tip: the section title is also the index entry for 'spread of fire'.","r":"300.21","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What type of wiring may go in a plenum above a suspended ceiling?","k":["Plenums","Ducts","Environmental air spaces","Wiring methods"],"ka":0,"s":["300.21 Spread of Fire or Products of Combustion","300.37 Aboveground Wiring Methods","300.4 Protection Against Physical Damage","300.22 Wiring in Ducts Not Used for Air Handling, Fabricated Ducts, and Other Spaces for Environmental Air (Plenums)"],"sa":3,"e":"300.22(C) lists permitted methods in other spaces used for environmental air, such as above a ceiling that is a return-air plenum: metal raceways, MC cable with a metal sheath, and cables listed for plenum use. NM cable is not allowed. Tip: 'Plenums' is a direct index entry.","r":"300.22(C)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the ampacity of flexible cord 12 AWG used for a portable heater?","k":["Extension cords","Cord ampacity","Portable cords","Flexible cords and cables"],"ka":3,"s":["240.5 Protection of Flexible Cords, Flexible Cables, and Fixture Wires","400.5 Ampacities for Flexible Cords and Cables","402.5 Allowable Ampacities for Fixture Wires","400.12 Uses Not Permitted"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 400.5(A)(1) gives the allowable ampacity of flexible cords. The index entry 'Flexible cords and cables' lists ampacities under 400.5. Tip: flexible cord is Art. 400, but fixture wires (luminaire wiring) are Art. 402.","r":"Table 400.5(A)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May flexible cord be run through a wall to feed a fixed appliance?","k":["Walls","Cord and plug connections","Appliances","Flexible cords and cables"],"ka":3,"s":["400.5 Ampacities for Flexible Cords and Cables","400.12 Uses Not Permitted","400.10 Uses Permitted","422.16 Flexible Cords"],"sa":1,"e":"400.12 prohibits flexible cords from being used as a substitute for fixed wiring, or from being run through holes in walls, ceilings or floors, doorways, windows, or concealed behind walls. Tip: if the question says 'may not', go to the 'Uses Not Permitted' section.","r":"400.12","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the maximum height of a wall switch's operating handle?","k":["Mounting height","Accessibility","Snap switches","Switches"],"ka":3,"s":["404.8 Accessibility and Grouping","404.14 Rating and Use of Switches","210.70 Lighting Outlets Required","404.2 Switch Connections"],"sa":0,"e":"404.8(A) requires all switches and circuit breakers used as switches to be located so they can be operated from a readily accessible place, with the center of the grip of the operating handle in its highest position not more than 6 ft 7 in. above the floor. Tip: the rule is the same for panelboards.","r":"404.8(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What does the Code say about lampholders and recessed lights in clothes closets?","k":["Storage space","Clothes closets","Closet lighting","Luminaires"],"ka":1,"s":["410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations","210.70 Lighting Outlets Required","410.116 Clearance and Installation","410.16 Luminaires in Clothes Closets"],"sa":3,"e":"410.16 defines the 'storage space' and requires clearances: 12 in. for surface-mounted incandescent or LED luminaires with a completely enclosed light source, 6 in. for recessed, 6 in. for surface-mounted LED or fluorescent. Tip: 'Clothes closets' appears in the index directly.","r":"410.16","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 4500-watt water heater is hardwired. Can its branch-circuit breaker serve as the appliance disconnect?","k":["Disconnect switches","Water heaters","Appliances, disconnecting means","Fixed storage-type water heaters"],"ka":2,"s":["422.31 Disconnection of Permanently Connected Appliances","422.11 Overcurrent Protection","422.30 General","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating"],"sa":0,"e":"For permanently connected appliances rated over 300 VA, 422.31(B) lets the branch-circuit switch or breaker serve as the disconnect if it is within sight of the appliance or can be locked in the open position. Tip: Art. 422 organizes the appliance rules by Part II to Part IV.","r":"422.31","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Do dishwashers and disposals have to be fed by cord or hardwired?","k":["Dishwashers","Garbage disposals","Appliances, flexible cords","Cord-and-plug-connected"],"ka":2,"s":["422.31 Disconnection of Permanently Connected Appliances","400.12 Uses Not Permitted","422.16 Flexible Cords","422.34 Unit Switch(es) as Disconnecting Means"],"sa":2,"e":"A built-in dishwasher or trash compactor may use a 3 to 4 ft cord (422.16(B)(2)); a food waste disposer may use an 18 in. to 36 in. cord (422.16(B)(1)). Both are optional, so hardwiring is also fine. Tip: appliances have their own flexible-cord section separate from Art. 400.","r":"422.16(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which appliances such as vending machines and water coolers have their own GFCI rule in the appliance article?","k":["Appliances","Vending machines","Water coolers","Ground-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":3,"s":["422.6 Listing Requirements","422.5 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","422.11 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":1,"e":"422.5 lists appliances needing GFCI protection for personnel, including cord-and-plug-connected vending machines, drinking water coolers, tire inflation machines, high-pressure spray washers and dishwashers in dwelling units, among others. Tip: the Art. 210 rule applies to receptacles; 422.5 applies to the appliance itself.","r":"422.5","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What size motor branch circuit conductors do I need for a 5-hp, 230-volt single-phase motor?","k":["Motors, branch-circuit conductors","Motor circuits","Conductor ampacity","Full-load current"],"ka":0,"s":["430.6 Ampacity and Motor Rating Determination","430.32 Continuous-Duty Motors","430.24 Several Motors or a Motor(s) and Other Load(s)","430.22 Single Motor"],"sa":3,"e":"Motor branch-circuit conductors are rated at 125 percent of the full-load current in Table 430.248, not the nameplate (430.6(A)(1)). A 5-hp, 230-volt single-phase motor is 28 A, so 28 x 1.25 = 35 A. Tip: Table 430.248 is single-phase and Table 430.250 is three-phase.","r":"430.22 and Table 430.248","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What overload protection should I set for a 3-hp motor with a 1.15 service factor and 14.0 A nameplate?","k":["Motor protection","Motors, overload protection","Service factor","Overload relays"],"ka":1,"s":["430.31 General","430.32 Continuous-Duty Motors","430.6 Ampacity and Motor Rating Determination","430.52 Rating or Setting for Individual Motor Circuit"],"sa":1,"e":"430.32(A)(1) sets separate overload devices at no more than 125 percent of nameplate full-load current for motors with a marked service factor of 1.15 or more, or temperature rise not over 40 C; for others it is 115 percent. For 14.0 A, 125 percent is 17.5 A. Tip: overload uses the nameplate; conductors and short-circuit use the table.","r":"430.32(A)(1)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the maximum size inverse-time breaker for a motor branch circuit?","k":["Motor circuits","Motors, branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protection","Inverse time breakers","Overcurrent protection"],"ka":1,"s":["430.32 Continuous-Duty Motors","430.52 Rating or Setting for Individual Motor Circuit","430.62 Rating or Setting - Motor Feeder Short-Circuit and Ground-Fault Protection","240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 430.52 allows an inverse-time breaker up to 250 percent of the full-load current from the motor tables; a dual-element time-delay fuse 175 percent; a nontime delay fuse 300 percent; an instantaneous-trip breaker 800 percent. 430.52(C)(1) Exception permits going to the next standard size if needed to start. Tip: the index entry is long but exact.","r":"430.52 and Table 430.52","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The disconnect for a motor is in a different room. Is that OK?","k":["Within sight","Disconnect switches","Motor controllers","Motors, disconnecting means"],"ka":3,"s":["430.101 General","430.102 Location","430.110 Ampere Rating and Interrupting Capacity","430.109 Type"],"sa":1,"e":"430.102(A) requires the disconnect to be in sight of the controller; 430.102(B) requires one in sight of the motor and driven machinery unless the controller disconnect can be locked open or the other exceptions apply. 'In sight' means visible and within 50 ft. Tip: Art. 430 Part IX covers disconnecting means.","r":"430.102","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What overcurrent protection is required on a 480-volt dry-type transformer that has protection on the primary side only?","k":["Secondary protection","Transformers, overcurrent protection","Overcurrent protection","Primary protection"],"ka":1,"s":["450.13 Accessibility","240.4 Protection of Conductors","450.3 Overcurrent Protection","450.9 Ventilation"],"sa":2,"e":"450.3(B) and Table 450.3(B) cover transformers of 1000 volts or less: primary-only protection is generally 125 percent of rated primary current (next size up allowed), and primary-plus-secondary protection permits 250 percent on the primary and 125 percent on the secondary. Tip: do not confuse it with 450.3(A) over 1000 volts.","r":"450.3(B) and Table 450.3(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How much clearance does a dry-type transformer need from combustible walls?","k":["Transformers, dry-type","Ventilation","Clearances","Transformers"],"ka":0,"s":["450.22 Dry-Type Transformers Installed Outdoors","450.21 Dry-Type Transformers Installed Indoors","450.9 Ventilation","450.13 Accessibility"],"sa":1,"e":"450.21(B) covers dry-type transformers of 112.5 kVA or less: they must be separated at least 12 in. from combustible material unless separated by a fire-resistant, heat-insulating barrier. Tip: Part II (450.21 and up) covers specific types.","r":"450.21(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A buyer asks if the panel is 'readily accessible' when it sits behind a locked door that only the owner can open. Where is the term defined?","k":["Accessible","Working space","Locked enclosures","Readily accessible"],"ka":3,"s":["404.8 Accessibility and Grouping","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","110.27 Guarding of Live Parts","Art. 100 Definitions"],"sa":3,"e":"Readily accessible means capable of being reached quickly for operation, renewal or inspection without having to climb over or remove obstacles or use portable ladders. Defined terms are indexed under the term, pointing to Art. 100. Tip: 'Accessible' (needs access but may need tools or obstacles removed) is a different definition, so choose the exact word.","r":"Art. 100 (Readily Accessible)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where does the NEC say what the 'service point' is?","k":["Service point","Service equipment","Utility connection","Service drop"],"ka":0,"s":["90.2 Scope","Art. 100 Definitions","230.2 Number of Services","230.24 Clearances"],"sa":1,"e":"The service point is the point of connection between the facilities of the serving utility and the premises wiring. It is defined in Art. 100, and the index lists it by name. Tip: where the service point sits determines what is a service conductor and what belongs to the utility.","r":"Art. 100 (Service Point)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the difference between an overload and an overcurrent, in NEC terms?","k":["Short circuit","Overcurrent","Overcurrent protection","Overload"],"ka":3,"s":["430.31 General","Art. 100 Definitions","110.9 Interrupting Rating","240.4 Protection of Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"Overload is operation of equipment in excess of normal full-load rating that, if it persists long enough, could cause damage or dangerous overheating. A short circuit or ground fault is not an overload. Overcurrent is the broader term. Look up each term to get its own Art. 100 definition.","r":"Art. 100 (Overload, Overcurrent)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Is a room with only a sink and a microwave a 'kitchen' under the NEC?","k":["Dwelling units","Small-appliance branch circuits","Kitchen","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 100 Definitions","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","210.11 Branch Circuits Required"],"sa":0,"e":"The NEC defines a kitchen as an area with a sink and permanent provisions for food preparation and cooking. That decides whether kitchen receptacle and GFCI rules apply. Tip: if a requirement depends on a room type, look the room up in Art. 100 first.","r":"Art. 100 (Kitchen)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the NEC definition of a separately derived system?","k":["Transformers","Separately derived system","Alternate power source","Grounding"],"ka":1,"s":["445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover","Art. 100 Definitions","250.30 Grounding Separately Derived Alternating-Current Systems","450.3 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":1,"e":"A separately derived system is a premises wiring system whose power is derived from a source other than a service, with no direct connection to circuit conductors of any other system other than through grounding and bonding connections. The definition is in Art. 100; the grounding rules are in 250.30. Tip: the question asks what it is, not how to ground it.","r":"Art. 100 (Separately Derived System)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What does the Code call the conductors between the service equipment and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device?","k":["Branch circuit","Conductors","Feeder","Service conductors"],"ka":2,"s":["230.2 Number of Services","210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","Art. 100 Definitions"],"sa":3,"e":"A feeder includes all circuit conductors between the service equipment, the source of a separately derived system, or other power supply source and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device. Tip: a branch circuit is defined in the same article, beginning at the final overcurrent device.","r":"Art. 100 (Feeder)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which definition tells me whether a covered porch is a 'wet location' or a 'damp location'?","k":["Weatherproof","Wet location","Damp location","Outdoor receptacles"],"ka":1,"s":["300.6 Protection Against Corrosion and Deterioration","Art. 100 Definitions","406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations","410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations"],"sa":1,"e":"Locations are defined in Art. 100: wet locations include installations underground or in concrete slabs in direct contact with the earth, and locations unprotected and exposed to weather; damp locations are partially protected locations such as under canopies and roofed open porches. Tip: look up both terms side by side.","r":"Art. 100 (Damp Location, Wet Location)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What does the NEC call a person who has skills and safety training to work on electrical equipment?","k":["Competent person","Authority having jurisdiction","Electrician","Qualified person"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 100 Definitions","110.2 Approval","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","90.4 Enforcement"],"sa":0,"e":"A qualified person has skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of the electrical equipment and installations and has received safety training on the hazards involved. The NEC does not use the words 'electrician' or 'competent person' as defined terms. Tip: scan the Art. 100 definitions for the word the Code actually uses.","r":"Art. 100 (Qualified Person)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"On a multiwire circuit I am replacing a receptacle. Why must the neutrals be pigtailed instead of passing through the device terminals?","k":["Receptacles","Neutral conductors, continuity","Multiwire branch circuits","Splices"],"ka":1,"s":["110.14 Electrical Connections","300.12 Mechanical Continuity - Raceways and Cables","210.4 Multiwire Branch Circuits","300.13 Mechanical and Electrical Continuity - Conductors"],"sa":3,"e":"300.13(B) requires the continuity of a grounded conductor on a multiwire branch circuit not to depend on device connections like receptacles, so a pigtail splice is needed. Tip: 'Continuity' in the index leads to 300.12 for raceways and cables, and 300.13 for conductors.","r":"300.13(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May I run 2 AWG conductors in parallel to carry a 200-amp feeder?","k":["Conductors, size","Paralleled feeders","Feeders","Parallel conductors"],"ka":3,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","310.10 Uses Permitted","215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","300.20 Induced Currents in Ferrous Metal Enclosures or Ferrous Metal Raceways"],"sa":1,"e":"Parallel conductors are covered in 310.10, which requires conductors in parallel to be 1/0 AWG or larger and to have the same length, material, size, insulation type and terminations. 2 AWG is too small. Tip: 'Parallel' is a direct entry, with subentries for grounding and conduit rules.","r":"310.10","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where do I find the rule on securing AC cable in a building?","k":["AC cable","Metal-clad cable","Cable supports","Armored cable"],"ka":3,"s":["320.10 Uses Permitted","330.30 Securing and Supporting","300.11 Securing and Supporting","320.30 Securing and Supporting"],"sa":3,"e":"Type AC cable is secured within 12 in. of every box and at intervals not exceeding 4 1/2 ft. The article is Art. 320; MC cable (Art. 330) has a 6 ft interval, so do not mix them. Tip: 'Armored cable' is the index word for Type AC.","r":"320.30","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How often must MC cable be strapped to the wall?","k":["Cable supports","Metal-clad cable","Armored cable","Type MC"],"ka":1,"s":["300.11 Securing and Supporting","320.30 Securing and Supporting","330.30 Securing and Supporting","330.10 Uses Permitted"],"sa":2,"e":"Type MC cable must be secured at intervals not exceeding 6 ft and within 12 in. of every box, cabinet, fitting or termination. Tip: Armored cable (AC) is every 4 1/2 ft, MC cable is every 6 ft.","r":"330.30","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How far apart do I strap flexible metal conduit on a wall?","k":["Greenfield","Conduit supports","Flexible metal conduit","FMC"],"ka":2,"s":["348.10 Uses Permitted","348.30 Securing and Supporting","300.11 Securing and Supporting","350.30 Securing and Supporting"],"sa":1,"e":"FMC must be securely fastened within 12 in. of each box or termination and at intervals not exceeding 4 1/2 ft. Liquidtight flexible metal conduit (Art. 350) uses the same distances. Tip: Art. 348 is FMC; Art. 350 is LFMC.","r":"348.30(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What percent fill can I put in a metal wireway?","k":["Conductor fill","Wireways","Metal wireways","Auxiliary gutters"],"ka":1,"s":["366.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9 Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","300.17 Number and Size of Conductors in Raceway","376.22 Number of Conductors and Ampacity"],"sa":3,"e":"In a metal wireway the sum of cross-sectional areas of all contained conductors at any cross section must not exceed 20 percent of the interior cross-sectional area. Conductors for signaling circuits or controller conductors between a motor and starter are not counted. Tip: Table 1 in Chapter 9 does not apply to wireways.","r":"376.22(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which section tells me how to size the circuit for a 5 kW electric baseboard heater?","k":["Space heating, fixed electric","Electric heating","Branch circuits","Heaters, baseboard"],"ka":0,"s":["424.19 Disconnecting Means","210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","424.3 Branch Circuits","422.13 Storage-Type Water Heaters"],"sa":2,"e":"424.3(B) requires fixed electric space-heating equipment branch-circuit conductors and overcurrent protection to be sized at not less than 125 percent of the total load of the heaters and motors. Tip: use the article for the equipment (Art. 424), not the general branch-circuit article.","r":"424.3(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"My 50-gallon electric water heater is 4500 watts at 240 volts. What ampacity do I size the branch circuit for?","k":["Storage-type water heaters","Water heaters","Branch circuits","Appliances"],"ka":1,"s":["422.13 Storage-Type Water Heaters","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating","422.31 Disconnection of Permanently Connected Appliances","424.3 Branch Circuits"],"sa":0,"e":"422.13 says a fixed storage-type water heater with a capacity of 120 gallons or less is treated as a continuous load, so the branch circuit is rated at least 125 percent of the nameplate rating. 4500 W / 240 V = 18.75 A, times 1.25 = 23.4 A, so a 30-amp circuit. Tip: water heaters are listed directly in the index.","r":"422.13","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Does a gas furnace with a blower motor need its own circuit?","k":["Central heating equipment","Branch circuits","Appliances","Furnaces"],"ka":0,"s":["424.3 Branch Circuits","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","422.12 Central Heating Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"422.12 requires central heating equipment other than fixed electric space-heating equipment to be supplied by an individual branch circuit; auxiliary equipment such as pumps, valves and humidifiers may be on the same circuit. Tip: the index lists 'Central heating equipment' straight away.","r":"422.12","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can I install a receptacle face-up in a kitchen countertop?","k":["Receptacles, mounting","Receptacle outlets, dwelling unit","Kitchens","Countertops"],"ka":0,"s":["406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations","210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","406.5 Receptacle Mounting","406.4 General Installation Requirements"],"sa":2,"e":"406.5(E) prohibits receptacles in dwelling unit countertops and similar work surfaces from being installed in a face-up position. Tip: 210.52(C) tells you where counter receptacles go; 406.5 tells you how they can be mounted.","r":"406.5(E)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The outdoor receptacle will be in an open weather-exposed location. What cover do I need?","k":["Outdoor receptacles","Weatherproof covers","Wet locations","Receptacles, damp or wet locations"],"ka":3,"s":["314.15 Damp or Wet Locations","406.4 General Installation Requirements","406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations","410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations"],"sa":2,"e":"406.9(B) requires 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in a wet location to have an enclosure that is weatherproof whether or not an attachment plug is inserted (an extra-duty in-use cover), and the receptacles must be listed weather-resistant. Tip: damp location rules appear in the same section.","r":"406.9","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 225-amp panelboard is fed by a 300-amp breaker. Is that OK?","k":["Panelboards","Feeders","Overcurrent protection","Main breaker"],"ka":0,"s":["240.4 Protection of Conductors","408.30 General","408.36 Overcurrent Protection","215.3 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":2,"e":"408.36 requires a panelboard to be protected on its supply side by overcurrent protection not greater than the panelboard's rating, with limited exceptions. A 300 A device on a 225 A panel violates it. Tip: panelboard rules are in Art. 408 Part III.","r":"408.36","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How close must the disconnect be to a condensing unit?","k":["Refrigerating equipment","Condensers","Disconnecting means","Air-conditioning"],"ka":3,"s":["430.102 Location","440.13 Cord-Connected Equipment","440.14 Location","210.63 Heating, Air-Conditioning, and Refrigeration Equipment Outlet"],"sa":2,"e":"Disconnecting means must be located within sight from and readily accessible from the air-conditioning or refrigerating equipment, and may not be on the equipment panel that hinders servicing. Tip: Art. 440 is in a parallel sequence to Art. 430 but its disconnect rule is its own.","r":"440.14","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Must the portable generator on the job have GFCI protection on its 120-volt outlets?","k":["Generators, portable","Temporary power","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Receptacles"],"ka":0,"s":["445.13 Ampacity of Conductors","445.20 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Receptacles on Portable Generators","445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover","590.6 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel"],"sa":1,"e":"445.20 requires GFCI protection on 125-volt and 125/250-volt 15- and 20-ampere receptacle outlets that are part of a portable generator, unless certain exceptions apply. Tip: 590.6 covers receptacles on temporary wiring, not receptacles on the generator itself.","r":"445.20","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What size conductors go from a 20 kW generator's terminals to the first overcurrent device?","k":["Conductors, generator","Ampacity","Overcurrent protection","Generators"],"ka":3,"s":["700.10 Wiring, Emergency Systems","445.13 Ampacity of Conductors","445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover","445.12 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":1,"e":"Generator output conductors must have an ampacity of at least 115 percent of the nameplate current rating of the generator. 20 kW at 240 V single phase is 83.3 A; times 1.15 is 95.8 A. Tip: the generator article has its own conductor-sizing rule.","r":"445.13(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where is the locked-rotor code letter marked on a motor and what does it tell me?","k":["Locked-rotor current","Nameplates","Code letters","Motors, marking"],"ka":3,"s":["430.110 Ampere Rating and Interrupting Capacity","430.8 Marking on Controllers","430.7 Marking on Motors and Multimotor Equipment","430.6 Ampacity and Motor Rating Determination"],"sa":2,"e":"430.7(B) requires motors of 1/2 hp and over to be marked with a locked-rotor indicating code letter on the nameplate, showing locked-rotor kVA per horsepower. Tip: if the question is about reading the motor nameplate, think 'marking'.","r":"430.7(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What ampere rating should the motor disconnect switch have for a motor with 28 A full-load current from the table?","k":["Disconnect switches","Motors, disconnecting means","Full-load current","Ampere rating"],"ka":1,"s":["430.109 Type","430.110 Ampere Rating and Interrupting Capacity","430.102 Location","430.52 Rating or Setting for Individual Motor Circuit"],"sa":1,"e":"The disconnecting means must have an ampere rating of at least 115 percent of the full-load current rating of the motor. 28 x 1.15 = 32.2 A, so use at least a 40-amp switch. Tip: ampere rating uses the table value; 430.109 covers the type of device.","r":"430.110(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The service disconnect is on the far side of the building. How close must it be to where the service conductors enter?","k":["Service entrance","Service equipment","Location of service disconnect","Service disconnecting means"],"ka":3,"s":["230.71 Maximum Number of Disconnects","225.32 Location","230.70 General","230.79 Rating of Service Disconnecting Means"],"sa":2,"e":"230.70(A)(1) requires the service disconnect to be installed at a readily accessible location either outside a building or inside nearest the point of entrance of the service conductors. Tip: 230.70 is the 'General' section of Part VI and covers where and how.","r":"230.70(A)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How must a service mast be supported to carry a service drop?","k":["Service-drop conductors","Weatherheads","Service masts","Rigid metal conduit"],"ka":2,"s":["230.54 Overhead Service Locations","230.26 Point of Attachment","230.24 Clearances","230.28 Service Masts as Supports"],"sa":3,"e":"Where a service mast supports service-drop conductors, it must be of adequate strength or supported by braces or guys, and all raceway fittings must be identified for use with service masts; only the service-drop conductors may attach to a mast. Tip: the index entry for masts goes directly there.","r":"230.28","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Is it okay to splice a grounding electrode conductor with a wire nut?","k":["Bonding","Grounding electrode system","Splices","Grounding electrode conductor"],"ka":3,"s":["250.68 Grounding Electrode Conductor and Bonding Jumper Connection to Grounding Electrodes","250.64 Grounding Electrode Conductor Installation","250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor","250.8 Connection of Grounding and Bonding Equipment"],"sa":1,"e":"250.64(C) requires the GEC to be installed in one continuous length without a splice or joint, except for irreversible compression connectors or exothermic welding, or busbars, so wire nuts are not allowed. Tip: 250.64 covers routing, protection and splices of the GEC.","r":"250.64(C)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can I re-identify a white 2 AWG feeder wire as an equipment ground by taping it green?","k":["Color coding","Conductors, identification","Equipment grounding conductors, identification","Green conductors"],"ka":2,"s":["310.120 Marking","200.6 Means of Identifying Grounded Conductors","210.5 Identification for Branch Circuits","250.119 Identification of Equipment Grounding Conductors"],"sa":3,"e":"Equipment grounding conductors are green, green with yellow stripes, or bare. 250.119(A) lets an insulated conductor larger than 6 AWG be permanently re-identified at each end and every accessible point, so the 2 AWG wire is allowed. Tip: 200.6 is for grounded (white or gray) conductors; 250.119 is for the green ones.","r":"250.119(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Where does the Code explain the 'grounding electrode system' and when I must use all the electrodes on site?","k":["Water pipe","Grounding electrode system","Ground rods","Grounding electrodes"],"ka":1,"s":["250.52 Grounding Electrodes","250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems","250.50 Grounding Electrode System","250.53 Grounding Electrode System Installation"],"sa":2,"e":"250.50 requires all electrodes described in 250.52(A)(1) through (A)(7) that are present at each building or structure served to be bonded together to form the grounding electrode system. If none exist, one of the electrodes in 250.52(A)(4) through (A)(8) must be installed. Tip: system is 250.50, list is 250.52, installation is 250.53.","r":"250.50","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The panel is on the outside wall of a house and the service neutral is to be bonded. Where does the main bonding jumper rule live?","k":["Grounded conductor","Neutral bonding","Main bonding jumper","Service equipment, bonding"],"ka":2,"s":["250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems","250.28 Main Bonding Jumper and System Bonding Jumper","250.30 Grounding Separately Derived Alternating-Current Systems","250.102 Grounded Conductor, Bonding Conductors, and Jumpers"],"sa":1,"e":"250.28 requires the main bonding jumper to be a wire, bus, screw or similar suitable conductor, and sized per Table 250.102(C)(1). The main bonding jumper connects the grounded conductor to the service equipment enclosure at the service only. Tip: the index lists it by name.","r":"250.28","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What are the connection methods allowed for ground clamps, and can I use a sheet-metal screw?","k":["Terminals","Grounding and bonding equipment, connection","Grounding electrode conductor","Ground clamps"],"ka":1,"s":["250.12 Clean Surfaces","250.8 Connection of Grounding and Bonding Equipment","110.14 Electrical Connections","250.70 Methods of Grounding and Bonding Conductor Connection to Electrodes"],"sa":1,"e":"250.8(A) lists permitted methods: listed pressure connectors, terminal bars, exothermic welding, machine screw-type fasteners that engage at least two threads or are secured with a nut, thread-forming machine screws that engage at least two threads, and others. 250.8(B) prohibits connection devices that depend on solder. Tip: 250.70 is the specific rule for connections to electrodes.","r":"250.8","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 4-wire feeder (two hots, neutral and ground) runs to a detached garage. Can the subpanel's neutral be bonded to its ground bar?","k":["Neutral conductors","Detached buildings","Subpanels","Grounded conductor, bonding"],"ka":3,"s":["250.28 Main Bonding Jumper and System Bonding Jumper","408.40 Grounding of Panelboards","250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems","250.32 Buildings or Structures Supplied by Feeder(s) or Branch Circuit(s)"],"sa":3,"e":"At a building supplied by a feeder, the grounded conductor is not connected to the equipment grounding conductor or grounding electrode, so a 4-wire feeder is required when there is an equipment grounding conductor (250.32(B)). 408.40 also requires the neutral and ground bars to be separate in subpanels. Tip: only the service and separately derived systems bond neutral to ground.","r":"250.32(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A paint shop stores and pumps gasoline vapors. Is that a Class I, Class II or Class III area, and where does the Code explain the classes?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Explosion-proof equipment","Flammable liquids","Gasoline"],"ka":0,"s":["501.1 Scope","500.6 Material Groups","500.7 Protection Techniques","500.5 Classifications of Locations"],"sa":3,"e":"Class I locations are those where flammable gases or vapors may be present in quantities sufficient to produce explosive or ignitable mixtures; Class II is combustible dust; Class III is ignitable fibers or flyings. 500.5 sets out the classes and the Division 1 and Division 2 definitions. Tip: start at the 'Hazardous (classified) locations' entry, then choose the class.","r":"500.5","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"An engineer says the area classification drawings are missing for a paint mixing room. What does the Code require?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Documentation","Area classification","Drawings"],"ka":0,"s":["90.4 Enforcement","500.4 General","500.5 Classifications of Locations","110.3 Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing (Product Certification) of Equipment"],"sa":1,"e":"500.4(A) requires all areas designated as hazardous (classified) locations to be properly documented, with the documentation available to those authorized to design, install, inspect, maintain or operate electrical equipment at the location. Tip: Art. 500 begins with general requirements before the classifications.","r":"500.4(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A grain elevator has combustible dust in the air. Which article covers the wiring?","k":["Combustible dusts","Grain elevators","Hazardous (classified) locations","Class I locations"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 502 Class II Locations","Art. 501 Class I Locations","Art. 506 Zone 20, 21, and 22 Locations for Combustible Dusts, Combustible Fibers, and Flyings","Art. 503 Class III Locations"],"sa":0,"e":"Class II locations are hazardous because of combustible dust, so Art. 502 applies. Class I is gas or vapor and Class III is fibers or flyings, as in a textile mill. Tip: the Zone 20, 21, 22 system in Art. 506 is an alternate for the same hazards, so check which system the facility uses.","r":"Art. 502","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A textile mill has lint and flyings that are easily ignitable but not likely to be in suspension. Which article applies?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Combustible dusts","Fibers and flyings","Textile mills"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 501 Class I Locations","Art. 503 Class III Locations","Art. 502 Class II Locations","Art. 500 Hazardous (Classified) Locations, Classes I, II, and III, Divisions 1 and 2"],"sa":1,"e":"Class III locations are hazardous due to easily ignitable fibers or flyings that are not likely to be in suspension in the air in quantities enough to produce ignitable mixtures. That is Art. 503. Tip: the keyword 'Fibers and flyings' is built into the Class III definition.","r":"Art. 503","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"When must a seal be installed on a conduit entering a Class I enclosure containing arcing parts?","k":["Drainage","Conduit seals","Explosion-proof equipment","Seals, Class I locations"],"ka":3,"s":["501.15 Sealing and Drainage","501.30 Grounding and Bonding","500.7 Protection Techniques","501.10 Wiring Methods"],"sa":0,"e":"501.15 requires a conduit seal within 18 in. of an enclosure that contains arcing or sparking devices in a Division 1 location, and for 2 in. trade size or larger conduit entering an enclosure that has terminals, splices or taps. It also covers drainage. Tip: the number to remember is 18 in.","r":"501.15(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What wiring methods are allowed in a Class I, Division 1 location?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Explosion-proof equipment","Conduit","Wiring methods, Class I locations"],"ka":3,"s":["500.8 Equipment","501.10 Wiring Methods","501.140 Flexible Cords","501.15 Sealing and Drainage"],"sa":1,"e":"501.10(A) lists the Division 1 methods: threaded rigid metal conduit or threaded steel IMC, Type MI cable with termination fittings, and certain Type MC-HL, PLTC and ITC cables in some situations. Tip: the Division 2 methods are in 501.10(B), which is longer.","r":"501.10(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What does the Code call a circuit that cannot release enough energy to ignite a hazardous atmosphere?","k":["Intrinsically safe systems","Hazardous (classified) locations","Energy-limited circuits","Class 2 circuits"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 505 Zone 0, 1, and 2 Locations","Art. 500 Hazardous (Classified) Locations, Classes I, II, and III, Divisions 1 and 2","Art. 504 Intrinsically Safe Systems"],"sa":3,"e":"Intrinsically safe systems are covered by Art. 504, which has its own wiring separation and identification rules. Class 2 circuits (Art. 725) are power limited for fire and shock, not for hazardous atmospheres. Tip: if the question mentions ignition capability, stay in Chapter 5.","r":"Art. 504","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Our plant uses the Zone system for classified areas. Where are the rules for Zone 1 and Zone 2 gas areas?","k":["Division 1","Hazardous (classified) locations","Class I locations","Zone classification"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 500 Hazardous (Classified) Locations, Classes I, II, and III, Divisions 1 and 2","Art. 506 Zone 20, 21, and 22 Locations for Combustible Dusts, Combustible Fibers, and Flyings","Art. 501 Class I Locations","Art. 505 Zone 0, 1, and 2 Locations"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 505 is the Zone system for flammable gases and vapors (Zones 0, 1 and 2); Art. 506 is the Zone system for combustible dusts and fibers. The Division method (Arts. 501-503) is an alternative. Tip: Zone 0 is gas present continuously, Zone 1 is likely in normal operation, Zone 2 is not likely.","r":"Art. 505","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where does the Code lay out how much of a commercial repair garage is a classified location?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Gasoline","Parking structures","Garages, commercial"],"ka":3,"s":["513.3 Classification of Locations","511.3 Area Classification, General","511.4 Wiring and Equipment in Class I Locations","514.3 Classification of Locations"],"sa":1,"e":"511.3 classifies repair garage areas, such as pits, areas below floor level and, where not adequately ventilated, the space up to 18 in. above the floor as Class I, Division 2. Ventilation changes the result. Tip: if the question asks where the classified area is, look at 511.3, not at the wiring rules.","r":"511.3","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Do receptacles in a commercial garage bay need GFCI protection?","k":["Service garages","Garages, commercial","Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":1,"s":["511.7 Wiring and Equipment Installed Above Hazardous (Classified) Locations","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","511.3 Area Classification, General","511.12 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel"],"sa":3,"e":"All 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles installed in areas where electrical diagnostic equipment, electrical hand tools or portable lighting equipment will be used must have GFCI protection. Tip: Art. 511 has its own GFCI rule, even though 210.8 also applies.","r":"511.12","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where must the emergency shutoff for a gas station's pumps be located?","k":["Emergency shutoff","Gasoline dispensers","Motor fuel dispensing facilities","Service stations"],"ka":2,"s":["514.11 Circuit Disconnects","514.9 Seals","514.3 Classification of Locations","514.8 Underground Wiring"],"sa":0,"e":"514.11(A) requires an emergency electrical disconnect at a location remote from the dispensers, not less than 20 ft and not more than 100 ft away, which shuts off all power to dispensing equipment. Tip: the Art. 514 index entry covers all fuel dispensing, so scan for 'disconnects'.","r":"514.11","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"An open paint spray booth needs wiring. Where do the classified areas and wiring rules appear?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Paint booths","Flammable finishes","Spray application, dipping, and coating processes"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping, Coating, and Printing Processes Using Flammable or Combustible Materials","Art. 501 Class I Locations","Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 516 gives the area classifications for spray booths, rooms and dip tanks and the wiring methods in them. It also references Art. 501 and 502 for equipment. Tip: the index term uses the process name rather than the hazard.","r":"Art. 516","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A hangar stores fueled aircraft. Which article gives the classified zones around the plane?","k":["Fuel tanks","Class I locations","Hazardous (classified) locations","Aircraft hangars"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 513 covers hangars where aircraft containing Class I or Class II liquids or gases are housed or serviced. It classifies the area around the aircraft and below floor level. Tip: some index terms are the same as the article title.","r":"Art. 513","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"How many receptacles must be at each bed in a hospital general care room?","k":["Patient bed location","Health care facilities","Hospitals","Hospital-grade receptacles"],"ka":1,"s":["517.13 Equipment Grounding Conductor for Receptacles and Fixed Electric Equipment in Patient Care Spaces","517.30 Essential Electrical Systems","517.18 General Care Spaces","517.19 Critical Care Spaces"],"sa":2,"e":"517.18(B) requires a minimum of four receptacles at each patient bed location, all listed hospital grade and identified as such, and 517.18(A) requires two branch circuits, with at least one from the emergency system. Tip: critical care (517.19) has more receptacles than general care.","r":"517.18(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What grounding method is required for receptacles in patient care spaces?","k":["Health care facilities","Hospital-grade receptacles","Equipment grounding conductors","Patient care spaces"],"ka":0,"s":["250.118 Types of Equipment Grounding Conductors","517.18 General Care Spaces","517.160 Isolated Power Systems","517.13 Equipment Grounding Conductor for Receptacles and Fixed Electric Equipment in Patient Care Spaces"],"sa":3,"e":"517.13 requires an insulated copper equipment grounding conductor sized per 250.122, in addition to a metal raceway or cable armor that qualifies as an equipment grounding return path. This redundant grounding applies to branch circuits serving patient care spaces. Tip: Art. 517 modifies Art. 250 for patient care.","r":"517.13","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Which section covers isolated power systems in operating rooms?","k":["Ground-fault protection","Isolated power systems","Operating rooms","Wet procedure locations"],"ka":1,"s":["517.160 Isolated Power Systems","250.30 Grounding Separately Derived Alternating-Current Systems","517.17 Ground-Fault Protection","517.20 Wet Procedure Locations"],"sa":0,"e":"517.160 covers isolated power systems, which are used where interruption of power from a ground fault could be hazardous, and requires a line isolation monitor. Isolated power can be used as protection in wet procedure locations under 517.20. Tip: the index lists Isolated power systems directly.","r":"517.160","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"An operating room is a wet procedure location. What protection is required for receptacles and equipment?","k":["Operating rooms","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Health care facilities","Wet procedure locations"],"ka":3,"s":["517.20 Wet Procedure Locations","517.19 Critical Care Spaces","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","517.160 Isolated Power Systems"],"sa":0,"e":"517.20(A) requires receptacles and fixed equipment within the area of the wet procedure location to be protected by GFCI or by an isolated power system, with an exception where interrupting the supply could be hazardous. Tip: the wet-procedure rules are in a separate section from critical care.","r":"517.20","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"How does the NEC define a category 1 patient care space?","k":["Patient care space","Health care facilities","Essential electrical systems","Critical care"],"ka":0,"s":["517.2 Definitions","Art. 100 Definitions","517.19 Critical Care Spaces","517.18 General Care Spaces"],"sa":0,"e":"Terms used in health care facilities, including patient care space categories (Category 1 is where failure is likely to cause major injury or death), are defined at the start of Art. 517 in 517.2, not in Art. 100. Tip: always check the definition section at the beginning of an article for special terms.","r":"517.2","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Which part of the Code covers a hospital's emergency and standby power arrangement?","k":["Emergency systems","Essential electrical systems","Health care facilities","Generators"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 700 Emergency Systems","517.30 Essential Electrical Systems","Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover"],"sa":1,"e":"517.30 begins the essential electrical system requirements for hospitals; it sets out the branches (life safety, critical, equipment) and how they connect to alternate power. Art. 700 is the general emergency system article, and Art. 517 amends it for health care. Tip: use the occupancy article first, then look at the general one.","r":"517.30","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A church hall holds about 150 people. Does it fall under the assembly occupancy article?","k":["Churches","Assembly occupancies","Auditoriums","Places of assembly"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 520 Theaters, Audience Areas of Motion Picture and Television Studios, Performance Areas, and Similar Locations","518.1 Scope","518.2 General Classification","90.2 Scope"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 518 applies to buildings or portions of buildings designed or intended for the gathering together of 100 or more persons for civic, political, travel, religious, social or recreational purposes, such as halls, churches and restaurants. Tip: the occupancy article's scope section tells you whether it applies.","r":"518.1","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where are the rules for dimmers and stage lighting in a school auditorium with a stage?","k":["Theaters","Assembly occupancies","Dimmers","Stage lighting"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios and Similar Locations","Art. 520 Theaters, Audience Areas of Motion Picture and Television Studios, Performance Areas, and Similar Locations","Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 520 covers stage switchboards, stage lighting, footlights, dressing rooms and similar areas in theaters and places of performance. Art. 518 covers the audience area of an assembly occupancy. Tip: the more specific article takes precedence.","r":"Art. 520","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"At a county fair, the carnival ride cords and receptacles are outdoors. What GFCI protection is required?","k":["Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Carnivals","Fairs","Temporary installations"],"ka":1,"s":["590.6 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel","525.20 Wiring Methods","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","525.23 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection"],"sa":3,"e":"525.23 requires GFCI protection for personnel on all 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere non-locking receptacles used for disassembly and reassembly or readily accessible to the general public, and on equipment that is readily accessible to the general public. Tip: Art. 525 has its own GFCI rule, which comes before Art. 590.","r":"525.23","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A movie studio is using a sound stage with portable stage lighting. Where do the studio rules sit?","k":["Theaters","Motion picture and television studios","Studios","Assembly occupancies"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 525 Carnivals, Circuses, Fairs, and Similar Events","Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios and Similar Locations","Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","Art. 520 Theaters, Audience Areas of Motion Picture and Television Studios, Performance Areas, and Similar Locations"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 530 covers motion picture and television studios, sound stages, approved production facilities and exterior sets; the audience area of a studio falls under Art. 520. Tip: a keyword like 'studios' is a clue to the right article.","r":"Art. 530","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"In a dairy barn, what is required to keep stray voltage from affecting animals?","k":["Livestock","Stray voltage","Equipotential planes","Agricultural buildings"],"ka":3,"s":["250.104 Bonding of Piping Systems and Exposed Structural Metal","547.10 Equipotential Planes and Bonding of Equipotential Planes","547.5 Wiring Methods","680.26 Equipotential Bonding"],"sa":1,"e":"547.10 requires an equipotential plane in livestock confinement areas with concrete floors, bonded to the electrical grounding system with a solid copper conductor not smaller than 8 AWG. Tip: Art. 547 uses the same concept as pool bonding in 680.26.","r":"547.10","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What wiring methods are allowed in a damp, corrosive poultry house?","k":["Wiring methods","Agricultural buildings","Corrosive atmospheres","Poultry houses"],"ka":1,"s":["334.10 Uses Permitted","300.6 Protection Against Corrosion and Deterioration","352.10 Uses Permitted","547.5 Wiring Methods"],"sa":3,"e":"547.5 gives wiring methods for agricultural buildings that are excessively dusty, damp or corrosive, including Type UF, NMC, copper SE cable, jacketed MC cable, rigid nonmetallic conduit and liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit. It also requires fittings and enclosures that are corrosion-resistant. Tip: Art. 547 adjusts the Chapter 3 rules for farms.","r":"547.5","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where do I find the rules for the service equipment at a mobile home?","k":["Service equipment","Mobile home parks","Manufactured homes","Mobile homes"],"ka":3,"s":["550.32 Service Equipment","Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks","550.10 Power Supply","Art. 552 Park Trailers"],"sa":0,"e":"550.32 covers service equipment for a mobile home, including where it is located, how it is mounted and how it is grounded. Art. 550 also covers the power supply cord or feeder (550.10) and the calculated load. Tip: the index entry 'Mobile homes' leads to the whole article.","r":"550.32","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where do I find the rules for pedestals at an RV park?","k":["Campgrounds","Recreational vehicles","Mobile homes","RV parks"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 552 Park Trailers","Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks","Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes, and Mobile Home Parks","Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings, and Commercial and Noncommercial Docking Facilities"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 551 Part VI covers recreational vehicle park sites, site supply equipment, receptacles and load calculations. Mobile home parks are in Art. 550 instead. Tip: the index term 'Recreational vehicles' lists the park rules in the same entry.","r":"Art. 551 Part VI","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Where do I look up shore power receptacles and GFPE on a marina dock?","k":["Marinas and boatyards","Boat receptacles","Docks","Shore power"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks","Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings, and Commercial and Noncommercial Docking Facilities","Art. 553 Floating Buildings"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 555 covers the wiring and equipment in or adjacent to a marina or docking facility, including shore power receptacles and ground-fault protection of equipment. Floating buildings (Art. 553) are different. Tip: the index term 'Marinas' leads directly to it.","r":"Art. 555","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Which section requires GFCI protection for temporary wiring at a construction site?","k":["Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Temporary installations","Construction sites"],"ka":2,"s":["590.4 General","590.6 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","525.23 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection"],"sa":1,"e":"590.6(A) requires GFCI protection for personnel for all temporary-wiring receptacle outlets that are in use by personnel, including receptacles on construction-site power, with separate paragraphs for the receptacle voltage and ampere ratings. Tip: 210.8 applies to permanent wiring.","r":"590.6","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"How long can temporary holiday decorative lighting stay up?","k":["Holiday lighting","Decorative lighting","Time limit","Temporary installations"],"ka":3,"s":["590.2 All Wiring Installations","590.5 Listing of Decorative Lighting","590.3 Time Constraints","590.4 General"],"sa":2,"e":"590.3(B) allows temporary electrical power and lighting installations for holiday decorative lighting for a period not to exceed 90 days. Construction temporary wiring is permitted during the period of construction (590.3(A)). Tip: look for the 'time constraints' section in the temporary article.","r":"590.3(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"How far from the edge of a swimming pool can a receptacle be installed, and what protection does it need?","k":["Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Outdoor receptacles","Swimming pools","Receptacles"],"ka":2,"s":["210.52 Dwelling Unit Receptacle Outlets","680.26 Equipotential Bonding","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"Receptacles must be at least 6 ft from the inside walls of the pool; those between 6 ft and 20 ft from the inside wall must be GFCI protected. Pool rules are indexed under 'Swimming pools'. Tip: 'Receptacles' alone would send you through dozens of subentries.","r":"680.22(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"How high must a service drop be above a swimming pool?","k":["Overhead conductors","Swimming pools","Clearances","Service-drop conductors"],"ka":1,"s":["230.24 Clearances","225.18 Clearance for Overhead Conductors and Cables","680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment","680.9 Overhead Conductor Clearances"],"sa":3,"e":"Overhead service-drop and feeder conductors may not pass over a pool, diving structure or observation stand, and must be kept clear of them per Table 680.9(A). For insulated supply cables of 0 to 750 volts the clearance to the maximum water level is 22 1/2 ft. Tip: the pool article overrides the general clearance articles.","r":"680.9(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Which metal parts around an inground pool have to be tied into the bonding grid?","k":["Swimming pools","Pool decks","Equipotential planes","Bonding"],"ka":0,"s":["250.104 Bonding of Piping Systems and Exposed Structural Metal","547.10 Equipotential Planes and Bonding of Equipotential Planes","680.26 Equipotential Bonding","680.23 Underwater Luminaires"],"sa":2,"e":"680.26 requires the pool shell reinforcing steel, metal pool accessories, perimeter surfaces, pool water, pool equipment and structural metal within 5 ft to be bonded together using solid copper not smaller than 8 AWG. This is the equipotential bonding grid, which is not the same as grounding. Tip: bonding is covered in a separate section from the lights and receptacles.","r":"680.26","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where must the disconnect for the pool pump and heater be located?","k":["Pool pumps","Maintenance disconnect","Swimming pools","Disconnecting means"],"ka":2,"s":["404.8 Accessibility and Grouping","680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment","430.102 Location","680.12 Maintenance Disconnecting Means"],"sa":3,"e":"680.12 requires a disconnecting means for pool and spa equipment that is readily accessible and within sight of the equipment it serves, at least 5 ft horizontally from the inside walls of the pool, spa or hot tub unless separated by a permanent barrier. Tip: 'within sight' and '5 ft' are the numbers to remember.","r":"680.12","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What protection does a decorative outdoor fountain with submersible lights and pump need?","k":["Swimming pools","Fountains","Submersible equipment","Ground-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":1,"s":["680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment","682.15 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection","680.23 Underwater Luminaires","680.51 Luminaires, Submersible Pumps, and Other Submersible Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"Part V of Art. 680 applies to fountains. 680.51 requires luminaires, submersible pumps and other submersible equipment to be protected by GFCI or other means listed for the purpose, and addresses cord lengths and wiring. Tip: fountains are indexed separately from pools even though they sit in the same article.","r":"680.51","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"The new bathroom has a hydromassage bathtub. How is its circuit protected?","k":["Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Hydromassage bathtubs","Spas and hot tubs","Bathtubs"],"ka":1,"s":["210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","680.42 Outdoor Installations","680.71 Protection","680.43 Indoor Installations"],"sa":2,"e":"Hydromassage bathtubs and their associated electrical components must be on an individual branch circuit and protected by a readily accessible GFCI. Spa and hot tub rules are separate from bathtub rules. Tip: the index term is the product name, not 'bathtubs'.","r":"680.71","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where do I find the requirement for a disconnect on an outdoor electric sign?","k":["Outline lighting","Luminous signs","Disconnecting means","Signs"],"ka":3,"s":["600.7 Grounding and Bonding","600.5 Branch Circuits","600.6 Disconnects","404.8 Accessibility and Grouping"],"sa":2,"e":"600.6 requires each sign and outline lighting system to have a disconnecting means, in sight of the sign (or capable of being locked in the open position) and controlling all ungrounded conductors. Tip: 'Signs' leads to Art. 600; scan for the disconnect entry.","r":"600.6","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Does a new retail storefront need a dedicated circuit for an outside sign?","k":["Branch circuits","Outline lighting","Commercial buildings","Signs"],"ka":3,"s":["210.11 Branch Circuits Required","600.6 Disconnects","210.62 Show Windows","600.5 Branch Circuits"],"sa":3,"e":"600.5(A) requires each commercial building and each commercial occupancy accessible to pedestrians to have at least one outlet in an accessible location at each entrance to each tenant space for a sign or outline lighting. The sign branch circuit is rated at 20 amperes and may supply no other loads. Tip: the sign article has its own branch-circuit section.","r":"600.5","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where do I find the rules for grounding a neon sign transformer enclosure?","k":["Neon tubing","Signs","Grounding","Transformers"],"ka":1,"s":["410.44 Methods of Grounding","600.7 Grounding and Bonding","250.118 Types of Equipment Grounding Conductors","600.21 Ballasts, Transformers, Electronic Power Supplies, and Class 2 Power Sources"],"sa":1,"e":"600.7 requires metal parts of signs, outline lighting, equipment and enclosures to be grounded and bonded, using an equipment grounding conductor or a circuit wiring method that qualifies. Tip: for other luminaires, see Art. 410; for signs, Art. 600 governs.","r":"600.7","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Is a GFCI required for the receptacle in an elevator machine room?","k":["Machine rooms","Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Elevators"],"ka":3,"s":["620.23 Branch Circuits for Car Lighting, Receptacle(s), Ventilation, Heating, and Air-Conditioning","620.51 Disconnecting Means","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","620.85 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel"],"sa":3,"e":"620.85 requires 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles installed in pits, hoistways, on elevator car tops and in machinery spaces to be GFCI protected. Tip: Art. 620 has its own rule, separate from 210.8.","r":"620.85","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where must the disconnect for an elevator's drive motor be located?","k":["Machine rooms","Disconnecting means","Controllers","Elevators"],"ka":3,"s":["620.22 Branch Circuits for Car Lighting, Receptacle(s), Ventilation, Heating, and Air-Conditioning","620.51 Disconnecting Means","430.102 Location","620.5 Working Clearances"],"sa":1,"e":"620.51 requires a single means for disconnecting all ungrounded main power supply conductors for each unit, and it must be lockable in the open position and located where it is readily accessible to qualified persons, typically in the machine room or machinery space. Tip: Art. 620 is Chapter 6 special equipment, with its own disconnect rules.","r":"620.51","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What branch circuit does the car lighting in an elevator need?","k":["Car lighting","Lighting circuits","Branch circuits","Elevators"],"ka":3,"s":["620.51 Disconnecting Means","620.22 Branch Circuits for Car Lighting, Receptacle(s), Ventilation, Heating, and Air-Conditioning","620.23 Branch Circuits for Machine Room or Machinery Space Lighting and Receptacle(s)","210.11 Branch Circuits Required"],"sa":1,"e":"620.22(A) requires a separate branch circuit for the car lights, receptacles, accessories and ventilation, with the overcurrent device located in the machine room or machinery space; 620.23 covers the machine room lighting and receptacles. Tip: car circuits and machine-room circuits have separate sections.","r":"620.22","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Do I need a separate branch circuit for the electric vehicle charger in my customer's garage?","k":["Branch circuits","Electric vehicle charging","Receptacles","Garages"],"ka":1,"s":["625.41 Overcurrent Protection","625.43 Disconnecting Means","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","625.40 Electric Vehicle Branch Circuit"],"sa":3,"e":"625.40 requires each outlet installed for the purpose of charging electric vehicles to be supplied by an individual branch circuit, and no other outlets may be on it. Tip: the index entry 'Electric vehicle charging' leads you to Art. 625.","r":"625.40","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What size breaker should I use for a 40-amp electric vehicle charger on a continuous charge?","k":["Overcurrent protection","Electric vehicle charging","Continuous loads","Branch circuits"],"ka":1,"s":["210.20 Overcurrent Protection","625.41 Overcurrent Protection","625.42 Rating","625.40 Electric Vehicle Branch Circuit"],"sa":1,"e":"EV supply equipment is considered a continuous load, so overcurrent protection and branch-circuit ampacity must be at least 125 percent of the maximum load of the equipment (625.41). 40 x 1.25 = 50 A. Tip: for a 40 A charger you need a 50 A breaker and wiring rated for 50 A.","r":"625.41","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Does a receptacle installed for EV charging in a garage need GFCI protection?","k":["Electric vehicle charging","Garages","Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":0,"s":["625.44 Equipment Connection","625.54 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","625.40 Electric Vehicle Branch Circuit"],"sa":1,"e":"625.54 requires all receptacles installed for the connection of electric vehicle charging to have GFCI protection for personnel. Tip: Art. 625 has its own GFCI rule, and 210.8 also applies for garages.","r":"625.54","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"When is a separate disconnecting means required for EV charging equipment?","k":["Electric vehicle charging","Disconnecting means","Charging stations","Branch circuits"],"ka":0,"s":["625.54 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","625.43 Disconnecting Means","430.102 Location","625.40 Electric Vehicle Branch Circuit"],"sa":1,"e":"625.43 requires a disconnecting means for EVSE rated more than 60 amperes or more than 150 volts to ground, installed in a readily accessible location and capable of being locked in the open position. Tip: EVSE with cord-and-plug connection are covered in 625.44.","r":"625.43","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What does the Code say about the maximum voltage of a PV source circuit on a cold morning?","k":["Solar","Photovoltaic systems","Temperature correction","Maximum voltage"],"ka":1,"s":["690.8 Circuit Sizing and Current","690.7 Maximum Voltage","690.9 Overcurrent Protection","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts"],"sa":1,"e":"690.7 requires the maximum voltage of PV circuits to be calculated by multiplying the rated open-circuit voltage of each series-connected module by a correction factor based on the lowest expected ambient temperature, or using the manufacturer's coefficient. Tip: cold temperature raises voltage, so size to the lowest temperature.","r":"690.7","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"How do I size conductors from a PV array if each module has an Isc of 10 A?","k":["Short-circuit current","Conductor sizing","Solar","Photovoltaic systems"],"ka":3,"s":["690.7 Maximum Voltage","690.31 Wiring Methods","690.9 Overcurrent Protection","690.8 Circuit Sizing and Current"],"sa":3,"e":"690.8(A) sets maximum PV circuit current at 125 percent of the sum of the parallel module short-circuit currents (Isc x 1.25), and the circuit conductors and overcurrent device are sized at 125 percent of that value, so 10 x 1.25 x 1.25 = 15.6 A for one string. Tip: the double 125 percent is a common exam trap.","r":"690.8","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Which section requires a PV system on a building to have a rapid shutdown function for firefighters?","k":["Disconnecting means","Photovoltaic systems","Rapid shutdown","Firefighter safety"],"ka":1,"s":["690.13 Photovoltaic System Disconnecting Means","690.11 Arc-Fault Circuit Protection (Direct Current)","690.12 Rapid Shutdown of PV Systems on Buildings","705.20 Source Disconnecting Means"],"sa":2,"e":"690.12 requires controlled conductors outside the array boundary to be limited, within 30 seconds of rapid shutdown initiation, to not more than 30 volts, with other limits for the array itself. Tip: rapid shutdown is indexed directly.","r":"690.12","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where is the disconnecting means requirement for PV system equipment?","k":["Disconnecting means","Solar","Inverters","Photovoltaic systems"],"ka":3,"s":["690.13 Photovoltaic System Disconnecting Means","230.70 General","690.12 Rapid Shutdown of PV Systems on Buildings","705.20 Source Disconnecting Means"],"sa":0,"e":"690.13 requires a means to disconnect the PV system from all wiring systems, including power systems, energy storage systems and utilization equipment, that is readily accessible, lockable and marked. Tip: do not confuse it with rapid shutdown, which is a separate function.","r":"690.13","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Which section covers the DC wiring methods that may be used on a rooftop PV array?","k":["Photovoltaic systems","PV wire","Wiring methods","Solar"],"ka":0,"s":["690.9 Overcurrent Protection","690.31 Methods Permitted","338.10 Uses Permitted","690.8 Circuit Sizing and Current"],"sa":1,"e":"690.31 covers PV system wiring methods, including PV wire or PV cable for exposed array wiring, and other recognized raceway wiring methods inside buildings. It also covers the sunlight and temperature considerations. Tip: the 'Wiring methods' section is called 'Methods Permitted' in Part IV.","r":"690.31","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where do I find the power-source rules for a fire pump?","k":["Standby power","Fire pumps","Fire protection systems","Emergency systems"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","695.3 Power Source(s) for Electric Motor-Driven Fire Pumps","Art. 700 Emergency Systems","695.4 Continuity of Power"],"sa":1,"e":"695.3 lists the permitted sources, such as a service dedicated to the fire pump or an on-site standby generator, with a reliable alternate source in some cases. Art. 695 is separate from the emergency article because of fire pump reliability. Tip: the index entry is 'Fire pumps', and 695.4 covers continuity of power.","r":"695.3","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What are the rules for an arc welder's supply conductors?","k":["Resistance welders","Duty cycle","Arc welders","Welders, electric"],"ka":3,"s":["630.11 Ampacity of Supply Conductors","630.12 Overcurrent Protection","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","630.13 Disconnecting Means"],"sa":0,"e":"630.11 sizes arc welder supply conductors from the rated primary current on the nameplate multiplied by a factor based on duty cycle in Table 630.11(A), for individual welders. Tip: 'Welders, electric' lists the sections for arc welders and resistance welders.","r":"630.11","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection allowed for an arc welder?","k":["Overcurrent protection","Disconnecting means","Welders, electric","Arc welders"],"ka":2,"s":["240.4 Protection of Conductors","630.12 Overcurrent Protection","630.13 Disconnecting Means","630.11 Ampacity of Supply Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"630.12(A) requires each arc welder to have overcurrent protection rated or set at not more than 200 percent of its rated primary current (I1max), with separate rules for the supply conductors in 630.12(B). Tip: 630.13 requires a disconnecting means in the supply circuit.","r":"630.12","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where do I find the rules for sizing conductors that feed a crane or hoist motor?","k":["Industrial machinery","Motors","Conductor ampacity","Cranes and hoists"],"ka":3,"s":["610.14 Rating and Size of Conductors","610.31 Runway Conductors","670.4 Supply Conductors and Overcurrent Protection","430.22 Single Motor"],"sa":0,"e":"610.14 gives the ampacity of conductors for short-time rated crane and hoist motors in Table 610.14(A), based on the motor time rating (such as 15, 30 or 60 minutes). Tip: Art. 610 modifies Art. 430 for cranes.","r":"610.14","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"The shop's CNC machine is an industrial machine. Where is the nameplate data requirement?","k":["Nameplates","Control panels","Industrial machinery","Machine tools"],"ka":2,"s":["409.110 Marking","670.3 Machine Nameplate Data","110.21 Marking","430.7 Marking on Motors and Multimotor Equipment"],"sa":1,"e":"670.3(A) requires industrial machinery to be marked with the supply voltage, phase, frequency, full-load current, the maximum ampere rating of the short-circuit and ground-fault protective device, the ampere rating of the largest motor, and the short-circuit current rating. Tip: Art. 670 has its own marking rule.","r":"670.3","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where do I look for the requirements for a data center's information technology equipment room?","k":["Data centers","Information technology equipment","Computer rooms","Raised floors"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 647 Sensitive Electronic Equipment","Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing, Amplification, and Reproduction Equipment","Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 645 covers equipment, power-supply wiring, equipment interconnecting wiring and grounding of information technology equipment and systems, including information technology equipment rooms, with the disconnecting means requirements in 645.10. Tip: data and computer terms point to 'Information technology equipment'.","r":"Art. 645","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where is an irrigation machine's grounding and bonding covered?","k":["Motors","Agricultural buildings","Center pivot","Irrigation machines"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits, and Controllers","Art. 675 Electrically Driven or Controlled Irrigation Machines"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 675 applies to electrically driven or controlled irrigation machines, including center-pivot rigs, and to their wiring, grounding and disconnects. Tip: the index term 'Irrigation machines' goes right to the article.","r":"Art. 675","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"How quickly must the generator carry the load in an emergency lighting system after the normal power fails?","k":["Exit lights","Transfer equipment","Emergency systems","Generators"],"ka":2,"s":["700.12 General Requirements","701.12 General Requirements","700.5 Transfer Equipment","702.12 Outdoor Generator Sets"],"sa":0,"e":"700.12 requires emergency system equipment to be arranged so that power is available within 10 seconds of loss of the normal supply. Legally required standby (Art. 701) has a 60-second limit instead. Tip: Art. 700 emergency, 701 legally required, 702 optional.","r":"700.12","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Can the emergency circuits run in the same conduit as the normal lighting circuits?","k":["Raceways","Emergency systems","Wiring methods","Exit signs"],"ka":1,"s":["700.16 Emergency Illumination","700.12 General Requirements","300.3 Conductors","700.10 Wiring, Emergency System"],"sa":3,"e":"700.10(B) requires emergency circuit wiring to be kept entirely independent of all other wiring and equipment, and not to enter the same raceway, cable, box or cabinet with other wiring, with a few exceptions (such as in transfer equipment enclosures). Tip: 300.3(B) does not override it.","r":"700.10(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What does the Code require for the battery in a self-contained emergency light fixture in a hallway?","k":["Unit equipment","Exit lights","Emergency systems","Battery-powered lighting"],"ka":2,"s":["700.10 Wiring, Emergency System","700.5 Transfer Equipment","700.16 Emergency Illumination","700.12 General Requirements"],"sa":3,"e":"700.12(F) covers unit equipment, which includes a rechargeable battery, charging means, lamps and a relay. The battery must be able to supply and maintain at least 87.5 percent of nominal battery voltage for a period of at least 1 1/2 hours (90 minutes). Tip: unit equipment is a subentry under emergency systems.","r":"700.12(F)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What must a transfer switch do in an emergency system?","k":["Emergency systems","Transfer equipment","Automatic transfer switch","Generators"],"ka":0,"s":["700.5 Transfer Equipment","445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover","701.5 Transfer Equipment","702.5 Transfer Equipment"],"sa":0,"e":"700.5 requires transfer equipment to be automatic, identified for emergency use and approved by the authority having jurisdiction, and to prevent inadvertent interconnection of normal and emergency sources. Tip: Art. 702 transfer equipment may be manual, but Art. 700 must be automatic.","r":"700.5","d":2,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A water treatment plant has a legally required standby generator. How long can the transfer take?","k":["Emergency systems","Standby power","Generators","Legally required standby systems"],"ka":3,"s":["702.12 Outdoor Generator Sets","701.12 General Requirements","700.12 General Requirements","701.5 Transfer Equipment"],"sa":1,"e":"701.12 requires legally required standby power to be available within 60 seconds of loss of the normal source. Emergency systems must be available within 10 seconds. Tip: the legally required system is mandated by government codes for things like smoke control or sewage disposal.","r":"701.12","d":2,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"I want to add a portable generator inlet to a house so the owner can run circuits during an outage. What article governs the transfer arrangement?","k":["Generators","Optional standby systems","Portable generators","Transfer switches"],"ka":1,"s":["702.5 Transfer Equipment","700.5 Transfer Equipment","702.7 Signs","445.18 Disconnecting Means and Shutdown of Prime Mover"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 702 covers optional standby systems, and 702.5 requires transfer equipment to prevent inadvertent interconnection of power sources, whether manual or automatic, so a back-feed to the utility cannot occur. Tip: the optional standby article is for loads that are not legally required.","r":"702.5","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A customer wants rooftop solar tied to the utility grid. Which article covers connecting a PV system to the premises wiring?","k":["Backfeed","Utility-interactive inverters","Interconnected electric power production sources","Photovoltaic systems"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic (PV) Systems","Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 705 covers the installation of one or more electric power production sources operating in parallel with a primary source, including the point of connection to the service, supply-side and load-side connections, and the 120 percent busbar rule. Art. 690 covers the PV equipment itself. Tip: the two articles work together.","r":"Art. 705","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Where do the NEC rules for a home battery backup system appear?","k":["Batteries","Solar","Energy storage systems","Backup power"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic (PV) Systems","Art. 480 Storage Batteries","Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 706 covers energy storage systems, including battery systems, flow batteries and capacitors, with rules for disconnects, overcurrent protection and listing. Art. 480 covers stationary batteries in general. Tip: 'Energy storage systems' is the index term and the article title.","r":"Art. 706","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Can a thermostat cable share a conduit with 120-volt line-voltage wiring?","k":["Separation of circuits","Class 2 circuits","Low-voltage wiring","Thermostat wiring"],"ka":1,"s":["725.136 Separation from Electric Light, Power, Class 1, Non-Power-Limited Fire Alarm, and Medium-Power Network-Powered Broadband Communications Circuits","725.121 Power Sources for Class 2 and Class 3 Circuits","760.136 Separation from Electric Light, Power, Class 1, Non-Power-Limited Fire Alarm, and Medium-Power Network-Powered Broadband Communications Circuits","300.3 Conductors"],"sa":0,"e":"Class 2 and Class 3 conductors generally must not share a cable, raceway or enclosure with light, power or Class 1 conductors, with limited exceptions such as when they are separated by a barrier or terminate inside equipment. Tip: low-voltage wiring rules are in Art. 725.","r":"725.136","d":2,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What kind of transformer or power supply may supply a doorbell or Class 2 control circuit?","k":["Low-voltage wiring","Doorbells","Class 2 circuits","Transformers"],"ka":2,"s":["725.41 Class 1 Circuit Classifications and Power Source Requirements","725.121 Power Sources for Class 2 and Class 3 Circuits","450.3 Overcurrent Protection","411.4 Listing Required"],"sa":1,"e":"725.121 says the power source for a Class 2 or Class 3 circuit must be a listed Class 2 or Class 3 transformer, a listed Class 2 or Class 3 power supply, or other listed equipment marked to identify the Class 2 or Class 3 power source. Tip: the power limits in Chapter 9 Tables 11(A) and 11(B) support this.","r":"725.121","d":2,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A hospital needs a critical operations power system. Which article gives those requirements?","k":["Emergency systems","Critical operations power systems","Standby power","Essential electrical systems"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","Art. 708 Critical Operations Power Systems (COPS)","Art. 700 Emergency Systems","Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 708 covers critical operations power systems, which provide power for designated critical operations areas whose failure would disrupt national security, the economy or public health and safety. It applies where the authority designates it. Tip: COPS is an addition to the emergency and standby articles.","r":"Art. 708","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Where do I find rules on a stand-alone solar and battery system that has no utility connection?","k":["Photovoltaic systems","Stand-alone systems","Off-grid systems","Energy storage systems"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic (PV) Systems","Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 710 covers power production systems that operate in stand-alone mode and are not connected to an electric production and distribution network. Art. 705 is for interconnected systems. Tip: 'Off-grid' is not an index term, but 'Stand-alone systems' is.","r":"Art. 710","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Where do I find the rules for grounding a TV antenna mast?","k":["Grounding","Antennas","Masts","Radio and television equipment"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","250.94 Bonding for Other Systems","Art. 810 Radio and Television Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 810 covers receiving and transmitting antennas and their lead-in conductors, mast grounding, and clearances from power lines. CATV (Art. 820) is a cable system, not an antenna system. Tip: 'Antennas' is an index entry that leads to Art. 810.","r":"Art. 810","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A cable TV drop comes into a house. Where is the grounding and bonding of the coaxial cable shield covered?","k":["Grounding","Coaxial cable","Community antenna television systems","Cable TV"],"ka":2,"s":["820.100 Cable Bonding and Grounding","Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","250.94 Bonding for Other Systems","810.21 Grounding Conductors - Receiving Stations"],"sa":0,"e":"820.100 requires the shield of the coaxial cable to be bonded or grounded as close to the point of entrance as practicable, with a grounding conductor not smaller than 14 AWG. The conductor must run to the intersystem bonding termination or grounding electrode system. Tip: use the cable system article, then the 100-series section for bonding.","r":"820.100","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What article covers wiring for telephone and data lines inside a building?","k":["Data cabling","Low-voltage wiring","Communications circuits","Telephone wiring"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems"],"sa":1,"e":"Chapter 8 contains the communications articles, which stand on their own and supplement or modify Chapters 1 through 7 only where they say so (90.3). Art. 800 covers general requirements for communications systems, including premises telephone and data wiring. Tip: do not apply Chapter 3 wiring methods to Chapter 8 unless the article references them.","r":"Art. 800","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"Does the general Chapter 3 wiring methods article apply to communications wiring?","k":["Wiring methods","Chapters","Code, arrangement of","Communications circuits"],"ka":2,"s":["110.3 Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing (Product Certification) of Equipment","90.2 Scope","90.3 Code Arrangement","Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems"],"sa":2,"e":"90.3 explains that Chapters 1, 2, 3 and 4 apply generally; Chapters 5, 6 and 7 supplement or modify the general rules; and Chapter 8 is not subject to the requirements of Chapters 1 through 7 except where they are specifically referenced. Tip: Chapter 9 tables apply only when referenced.","r":"90.3","d":3,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What percent of a conduit's cross-section may be filled with three or more conductors?","k":["Raceways, number of conductors in","Conductor fill, boxes","Conduit bodies","Boxes, number of conductors"],"ka":0,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","300.17 Number and Size of Conductors in Raceway","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":0,"e":"300.17 sends you to Chapter 9 for conduit fill. Table 1 sets the maximum fill at 53 percent for one conductor, 31 percent for two, and 40 percent for over two conductors. Tip: the percentage table is Table 1; the conduit area table is Table 4; the wire area table is Table 5.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 1","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"A single 4/0 conductor is pulled through a short conduit run. What is the maximum fill percentage?","k":["Raceways, number of conductors in","Pull boxes","Conductors, size","Conduit fill"],"ka":0,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","314.28 Pull and Junction Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 1 gives 53 percent for one conductor, 31 percent for two, and 40 percent for more than two. The raceway article (such as 358.22 for EMT) refers you to Chapter 9. Tip: always look at the notes below the table, since they cover nipples and multiconductor cables.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 1","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"A 20-in. conduit nipple joins two enclosures. How full can it be?","k":["Conduit bodies","Conduit fill","Raceways, number of conductors in","Pull boxes"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","366.22 Number of Conductors","300.17 Number and Size of Conductors in Raceway"],"sa":0,"e":"Note 4 to Chapter 9 Table 1 permits a conduit or tubing nipple having a maximum length not to exceed 24 in. to be filled to 60 percent of its total cross-sectional area, and the ampacity adjustment factors need not apply. Tip: read the notes at the end of Chapter 9 before the tables.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 1, Note 4","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the cross-sectional area of one 12 AWG THHN conductor?","k":["Wire gauge","Conductors, dimensions","Conductors, insulated","Conductors, size"],"ka":1,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","310.104 Conductor Constructions and Applications","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":3,"e":"Table 5 lists the approximate area in square inches for each insulation type and size; 12 AWG THHN is 0.0133 sq in. Use it with Table 4 for conduit fill with different-size wires. Tip: Table 8 is for bare conductor properties, not insulated dimensions.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 5","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How many square inches of fill does a 3/4 in. EMT allow for more than two conductors?","k":["Conduit, dimensions","Raceways, dimensions","Electrical metallic tubing","Conduit fill"],"ka":1,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","358.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 4 lists the dimensions and percent area of each conduit type; for 3/4 in. EMT, the 40 percent column gives 0.213 sq in. Pick the table section for the correct raceway article (here, Article 358). Tip: Table 4 is organized by article, so find 'Article 358 EMT' first.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 4 (Article 358)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How many 12 AWG THHN conductors can go in a 1/2 in. EMT?","k":["Electrical metallic tubing","Raceways, number of conductors in","Conduit and tubing fill tables","Conductors, size"],"ka":2,"s":["358.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size"],"sa":3,"e":"Annex C, Table C.1 lists the maximum number of same-size conductors in EMT; for 12 AWG THHN in a 1/2 in. trade size the table gives 9. That answer matches Table 4's 40 percent area (0.122 sq in.) divided by 0.0133 sq in. Tip: Annex C is only for conductors of the same size and insulation type.","r":"Annex C, Table C.1","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How many 10 AWG THHN conductors fit in 3/4 in. EMT?","k":["Conductor sizes","Conduit fill","Electrical metallic tubing","Raceways, number of conductors in"],"ka":3,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","358.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables"],"sa":1,"e":"Table C.1 in Annex C gives 10 conductors of 10 AWG THHN in 3/4 in. EMT. You can also divide the 0.213 sq in. 40 percent area by 0.0211 sq in. Tip: the Annex C tables save time when every conductor is the same size.","r":"Annex C, Table C.1","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"I have three 10 AWG, two 12 AWG and one 14 AWG THHN in one conduit. Which table gives the area of each wire so I can add them up?","k":["Conduit fill, different sizes","Conductor area","Conductors, dimensions","Annex C"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":3,"e":"For mixed sizes, add up the individual areas from Table 5 (THHN 10 AWG 0.0211, 12 AWG 0.0133, 14 AWG 0.0097 sq in.) and compare the total with the 40 percent column in Table 4. Annex C works only for conductors of the same size. Tip: Table 5 is for insulated conductors.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 5","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How do I calculate the voltage drop on a long 3-phase feeder using the NEC's ac impedance values?","k":["Voltage drop","Conductors, size","Conductors, alternating-current resistance and reactance","Impedance"],"ka":2,"s":["215.2 Minimum Rating and Size","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts"],"sa":2,"e":"Table 9 gives ac resistance and reactance per 1000 ft for 600-volt cables, including values for effective impedance (Z) at 0.85 power factor. Use it with load current and length to find voltage drop. Tip: Table 8 gives dc resistance, so it is not the right table for ac feeders.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 9","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the circular mil area of a 10 AWG conductor?","k":["Conductors, size","Wire gauge","Circular mils","Conductors, properties"],"ka":3,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 8 lists conductor properties including area in circular mils; 10 AWG is 10,380 cmil. It also lists dc resistance and stranding. Tip: Table 5 is for finished-conductor dimensions with insulation, Table 8 for the metal itself.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 8","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How many strands does a Class B 1/0 AWG copper conductor have?","k":["Stranded conductors","Conductors, properties","Circular mils","Conductors, size"],"ka":1,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","310.104 Conductor Constructions and Applications"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 8 lists conductor properties, including the number of strands, diameter, area and dc resistance. A Class B 1/0 AWG conductor has 19 strands. Tip: the table title says 'Conductor Properties', so use it for any physical property of the bare conductor.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 8","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the minimum radius for a field bend in 1 in. rigid metal conduit?","k":["Bends","Conduit and tubing bends, radius","Raceways, bending radius","Rigid metal conduit"],"ka":1,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 2 Radius of Conduit and Tubing Bends","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","358.24 Bends - How Made","344.24 Bends - How Made"],"sa":0,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 2 lists the minimum radius to the center of the conduit for one-shot and full-shaped shoe benders and for other bends, by trade size. 344.24 directs you to the table for RMC. Tip: the article says how the bend is made and refers to the Chapter 9 table for the numbers.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 2","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Is a 24-volt, 100 VA transformer feeding a thermostat circuit within the Class 2 power limits?","k":["Power source limitations","Transformers","Class 2 circuits","Thermostat wiring"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 11(A) Class 2 and Class 3 Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations","Chapter 9, Table 12(A) PLFA Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations","725.121 Power Sources for Class 2 and Class 3 Circuits","Chapter 9, Table 11(B) Class 2 and Class 3 Direct-Current Power Source Limitations"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 11(A) gives the output limits for Class 2 and Class 3 ac sources, such as voltage, current, VA and overcurrent protection. 725.121 tells you what power sources are acceptable and refers to these tables. Tip: Table 11(B) is the dc version; Table 12 is for fire alarm circuits.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 11(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where do I find the output limits for a power-limited fire alarm circuit?","k":["Power-limited circuits","Power source limitations","Fire alarm systems","Class 2 circuits"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 11(A) Class 2 and Class 3 Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations","760.121 Power Sources for Power-Limited Fire Alarm Circuits","760.41 Power Sources for NPLFA Circuits","Chapter 9, Table 12(A) PLFA Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations"],"sa":3,"e":"Tables 12(A) and 12(B) give the power source limitations for power-limited fire alarm (PLFA) circuits, with ac in 12(A) and dc in 12(B). 760.121 references them. Tip: Table 11 is for Class 2 and Class 3, so choose the table by circuit type.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 12(A)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"I have a terminal lug with no torque value marked. Where can I find a recommended torque?","k":["Lugs","Electrical connections","Tightening torque","Terminals"],"ka":2,"s":["Annex D Examples","Annex A Product Safety Standards","110.14 Electrical Connections","Annex I Recommended Tightening Torque Tables from UL Standard 486A-486B"],"sa":3,"e":"Annex I lists recommended tightening torques for screws, bolts and slotted head terminal screws, based on UL 486A-486B, for use when the manufacturer does not give a value. 110.14(D) requires a calibrated torque tool when a value is provided. Tip: this is an informational annex, not a requirement.","r":"Annex I","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where can I see a worked example of a standard-method dwelling unit load calculation?","k":["Examples","Calculations","Dwelling units","Load calculations, dwelling units"],"ka":0,"s":["Annex D Examples","220.82 Dwelling Unit - Optional Calculation","Annex B Application Information for Ampacity Calculation","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables"],"sa":0,"e":"Annex D has worked examples, beginning with Example D1(a), a one-family dwelling with a standard calculation, and others for multifamily dwellings, stores and motors. The annex is informational only. Tip: Annex D is the one to read when your answers do not match the calculation.","r":"Annex D, Example D1(a)","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where do I find the engineering method for the ampacity of cables in a duct bank?","k":["Duct banks","Underground installations","Conductors, ampacity","Ampacity calculation, application information"],"ka":3,"s":["Annex D Examples","Annex B Application Information for Ampacity Calculation","300.5 Underground Installations","Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts"],"sa":1,"e":"Annex B has the information and tables for ampacities of conductors in duct banks and other special conditions, for use under engineering supervision. It is informative and is not a code requirement unless the AHJ adopts it. Tip: Table 310.16 is the starting point for the most common cases.","r":"Annex B","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What standard applies to molded-case circuit breakers that the NEC refers to?","k":["UL standards","Product safety standards","Circuit breakers","Listing"],"ka":1,"s":["Annex A Product Safety Standards","Annex I Recommended Tightening Torque Tables from UL Standard 486A-486B","110.3 Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing (Product Certification) of Equipment","240.6 Standard Ampere Ratings"],"sa":0,"e":"Annex A lists product safety standards used for product listing, such as UL 489 for molded-case circuit breakers, and the NEC sections where each is referenced. It is an informative annex. Tip: this Annex tells you which standard to ask for in a spec.","r":"Annex A, Table A.1","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What does 'Type II construction' mean when the Code refers to building construction?","k":["Combustible construction","Fire-resistive construction","Types of construction","Building construction"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 100 Definitions","Annex D Examples","300.21 Spread of Fire or Products of Combustion","Annex E Types of Construction"],"sa":3,"e":"Annex E gives the types of construction (Type I through V) as defined in NFPA 220 and used where the Code refers to the fire-resistance of a building. It helps decide fire-resistance categories. Tip: Annex E is informative only.","r":"Annex E","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where are the rules for accessibility heights for equipment per ADA requirements shown in the NEC?","k":["Americans with Disabilities Act","Handicap access","Accessibility","Mounting height"],"ka":0,"s":["110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","Annex H Administration and Enforcement Example","Annex J ADA Standards for Accessible Design","404.8 Accessibility and Grouping"],"sa":2,"e":"Annex J contains excerpts of the ADA Standards for Accessible Design as informative guidance for the electrical installer. 'Accessible' in Art. 100 is a different meaning, relating to access to equipment. Tip: the ADA reach range is separate from the NEC's own mounting heights.","r":"Annex J","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where can a jurisdiction find a sample of how to administer and enforce the NEC?","k":["Administration and enforcement","Authority having jurisdiction","Inspection","Enforcement"],"ka":0,"s":["90.4 Enforcement","Annex D Examples","Annex H Administration and Enforcement Example","110.2 Approval"],"sa":2,"e":"Annex H is a model of administration and enforcement provisions that a jurisdiction may use, including a permit and inspection process. It is not part of the Code. Tip: 90.4 tells you who enforces; Annex H shows how a jurisdiction could do it.","r":"Annex H","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where does the Code discuss supervisory control and data acquisition systems?","k":["Supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA)","Remote monitoring","Control systems","Industrial controls"],"ka":0,"s":["Annex F Availability and Reliability for Critical Operations Power Systems; and Development and Implementation of Functional Performance Tests (FPTs) for Critical Operations Power Systems","Annex G Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA)","Art. 708 Critical Operations Power Systems (COPS)","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits"],"sa":1,"e":"Annex G covers SCADA systems, which are used for monitoring and control in critical operations power systems and similar installations. It is informative. Tip: Annex F covers availability and reliability of COPS, so both annexes sit near Art. 708.","r":"Annex G","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Where do I find guidance for testing a critical operations power system?","k":["Functional performance tests","Emergency systems","Critical operations power systems","Reliability"],"ka":2,"s":["700.12 General Requirements","Art. 708 Critical Operations Power Systems (COPS)","Annex F Availability and Reliability for Critical Operations Power Systems; and Development and Implementation of Functional Performance Tests (FPTs) for Critical Operations Power Systems","Annex G Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA)"],"sa":2,"e":"Annex F gives information for the availability and reliability of COPS and a method of developing functional performance tests. It is informational, while Art. 708 is the mandatory text. Tip: Annex F is the best match when the question says reliability or functional tests.","r":"Annex F","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"A 3-inch rigid metal conduit has five 500 kcmil conductors. Which table lists the conduit's inside area?","k":["Conduit, dimensions","Conduit fill","Raceways, dimensions","Rigid metal conduit"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","344.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 4 lists dimensions and percent areas by conduit type, with a section for Article 344 (RMC). Combine it with Table 5 for the 500 kcmil conductor area and Table 1 for the fill percentage. Tip: it takes all three tables for a fill check.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 4 (Article 344)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Which table has the cross-sectional area of a bare 6 AWG equipment grounding conductor, which must be counted in conduit fill?","k":["Bare wire","Conductors, bare, dimensions","Conduit fill","Equipment grounding conductors"],"ka":1,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","250.122 Size of Equipment Grounding Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 8 gives the area for bare conductors (6 AWG is 0.027 sq in. for stranded), and Chapter 9 notes require equipment grounding conductors to be included in the fill calculation. Table 5 covers only insulated conductors. Tip: for insulated EGCs, use Table 5.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 8 and notes to the tables","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"A 2000-amp switchboard is 8 ft wide. Does the working space need an exit at each end?","k":["Working space","Clearances","Switchboards","Egress"],"ka":0,"s":["110.27 Guarding of Live Parts","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","110.34 Work Space and Guarding","408.18 Clearances"],"sa":1,"e":"110.26(C)(2) requires one entrance of sufficient area at each end of the working space for equipment rated 1200 amperes or more and over 6 ft wide, containing overcurrent devices, switching devices or control devices. 110.26(C)(1) requires at least one entrance for smaller equipment. Tip: the lettered paragraph for entrance and egress is (C).","r":"110.26(C)(2)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Is a light required in the electrical room where the panels are installed?","k":["Lighting","Illumination","Electrical rooms","Working space"],"ka":3,"s":["210.70 Lighting Outlets Required","110.27 Guarding of Live Parts","700.16 Emergency Illumination","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"110.26(D) requires illumination in all working spaces about service equipment, switchboards, panelboards or motor control centers installed indoors, and the lighting must not be controlled only by automatic means. Tip: the lighting requirement is part of the working space section.","r":"110.26(D)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Do exposed live parts at 480 volts in an electrical room need guarding?","k":["Enclosures","Live parts","Guarding of live parts","Barriers"],"ka":2,"s":["110.31 Enclosure for Electrical Installations","110.26 Spaces About Electrical Equipment","110.30 General","110.27 Guarding of Live Parts"],"sa":3,"e":"110.27(A) requires live parts of electrical equipment operating at 50 volts or more to be guarded against accidental contact by approved enclosures, by location in a room or vault accessible only to qualified persons, by suitable permanent partitions or screens, or by elevation of 8 ft or more. Tip: the index entry uses the exact phrase 'Guarding of live parts'.","r":"110.27(A)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What size branch circuit and conductors do I need for a 12 kW household electric range?","k":["Ranges","Cooking equipment","Appliances","Branch circuits"],"ka":0,"s":["210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating","210.11 Branch Circuits Required","220.55 Electric Cooking Appliances in Dwelling Units and Household Cooking Appliances Used in Instructional Programs"],"sa":0,"e":"210.19(A)(3) covers household ranges of 8 3/4 kW or more: the branch circuit rating shall be not less than 40 amperes, and the conductors must have an ampacity of at least the rating of the branch circuit (and not less than the maximum load). A 12 kW range is calculated at 8 kW demand for feeder calculations, but the branch circuit is a separate question. Tip: the load calculation is in Art. 220; the branch circuit is in 210.19.","r":"210.19(A)(3)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"May a 15-amp duplex receptacle be installed on a 20-amp circuit with other receptacles?","k":["Outlet devices","Duplex receptacles","Branch circuits","Receptacles, rating"],"ka":3,"s":["210.23 Permissible Loads, Other Than Individual Branch Circuits","210.21 Outlet Devices","406.3 Receptacle Rating and Type","210.19 Conductors - Minimum Ampacity and Size"],"sa":1,"e":"210.21(B)(3) allows a 15-ampere or 20-ampere receptacle on a 20-ampere branch circuit that supplies two or more receptacles. A single receptacle on an individual branch circuit must have an ampere rating not less than the circuit (210.21(B)(1)). Tip: Table 210.21(B)(3) lists the combinations.","r":"210.21(B)","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 20-amp lighting and receptacle circuit also feeds a fixed bathroom heater. How large can the fixed appliance be?","k":["Branch circuits, permissible loads","Receptacle loading","Lighting circuits","Appliances, fixed"],"ka":0,"s":["210.21 Outlet Devices","210.23 Permissible Loads, Other Than Individual Branch Circuits","210.20 Overcurrent Protection","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating"],"sa":1,"e":"210.23(A)(2) says that where a 15- or 20-ampere branch circuit supplies lighting units, other utilization equipment, or a combination, the total rating of fixed appliances shall not exceed 50 percent of the branch-circuit rating, if lighting units or cord-and-plug-connected equipment not fastened in place are also supplied. On a 20 A circuit, that is 10 A. Tip: this rule is why heaters get their own circuit.","r":"210.23(A)(2)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What demand do I use for a dwelling clothes dryer in a service calculation?","k":["Load calculations","Clothes dryers","Appliance loads","Dryer load"],"ka":1,"s":["220.53 Appliance Load - Dwelling Unit(s)","220.55 Electric Cooking Appliances in Dwelling Units and Household Cooking Appliances Used in Instructional Programs","422.10 Branch-Circuit Rating","220.54 Electric Clothes Dryers - Dwelling Unit(s)"],"sa":3,"e":"220.54 says the load for household electric clothes dryers is 5000 watts or the nameplate rating, whichever is larger, for each dryer, with demand factors in Table 220.54 when there are multiple dryers. Tip: do not mix this up with appliances in 220.53 (75 percent demand with four or more appliances).","r":"220.54","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How do I figure the existing load when adding a new addition to a commercial building?","k":["Metering","Maximum demand","Load calculations, existing loads","Service upgrades"],"ka":2,"s":["220.40 General","230.42 Minimum Size and Rating","220.87 Determining Existing Loads","220.82 Dwelling Unit - Optional Calculation"],"sa":2,"e":"220.87 allows the maximum demand data for a 1-year period to be used to calculate the existing load, increased by 125 percent, plus the new load. A 30-day recording method with a 125 percent factor is also permitted. Tip: this is usually used with demand meters, not nameplate totals.","r":"220.87","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What size service-entrance conductors do I need for a continuous load plus noncontinuous load?","k":["Ampacity","Service-entrance conductors","Service conductors","Conductor size"],"ka":1,"s":["310.12 Single-Phase Dwelling Services and Feeders","230.79 Rating of Service Disconnecting Means","230.42 Minimum Size and Rating","215.2 Minimum Rating and Size"],"sa":2,"e":"230.42(A) requires service-entrance conductors to have an ampacity not less than the maximum load to be served, with the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load as the minimum. Dwelling services have a separate rule in 310.12. Tip: Art. 230 has its own size rule, parallel to 215.2 for feeders.","r":"230.42(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"When is ground-fault protection of equipment required on a 480Y/277-volt service?","k":["Ground-fault protection of equipment","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Solidly grounded wye systems","Service equipment"],"ka":0,"s":["215.10 Ground-Fault Protection of Equipment","240.87 Arc Energy Reduction","210.13 Ground-Fault Protection of Equipment","230.95 Ground-Fault Protection of Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"230.95 requires ground-fault protection of equipment for solidly grounded wye electric services of more than 150 volts to ground but not exceeding 1000 volts phase-to-phase for each service disconnect rated 1000 amperes or more. The maximum setting is 1200 amperes with a maximum delay of 1 second for ground-fault currents of 3000 amperes or more. Tip: 215.10 covers feeders, 230.95 covers services.","r":"230.95","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Which AC systems must be grounded?","k":["Grounded systems","Grounding","Ungrounded systems","Alternating-current systems"],"ka":0,"s":["250.24 Grounding Service-Supplied Alternating-Current Systems","250.21 Alternating-Current Systems of 50 Volts to 1000 Volts Not Required to Be Grounded","250.4 General Requirements for Grounding and Bonding","250.20 Alternating-Current Systems to Be Grounded"],"sa":3,"e":"250.20 requires AC systems of 50 to 1000 volts to be grounded where they can be grounded so the maximum voltage to ground does not exceed 150 volts, where the system is 3-phase 4-wire wye with the neutral used as a circuit conductor, and where it is 3-phase 4-wire delta with the midpoint of one phase used as a circuit conductor. Tip: the not-required list is in 250.21.","r":"250.20(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"How much concrete-encased steel do I need for a Ufer ground in a new footing?","k":["Reinforcing bars","Foundation grounds","Grounding electrodes","Concrete-encased electrodes"],"ka":2,"s":["250.66 Size of Alternating-Current Grounding Electrode Conductor","250.52 Grounding Electrodes","250.64 Grounding Electrode Conductor Installation","250.53 Grounding Electrode System Installation"],"sa":1,"e":"250.52(A)(3) describes the concrete-encased electrode: at least 20 ft of one or more bare or zinc-galvanized or other electrically conductive coated steel reinforcing bars or rods at least 1/2 in. in diameter, or at least 20 ft of bare copper conductor not smaller than 4 AWG, encased in at least 2 in. of concrete near the bottom of a foundation in direct contact with the earth. Tip: the list of electrode types is in 250.52(A).","r":"250.52(A)(3)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the rule on bolting a panelboard enclosure to a damp masonry wall?","k":["Wet locations","Panelboards","Cabinets and cutout boxes","Damp locations"],"ka":2,"s":["312.5 Cabinets, Cutout Boxes, and Meter Socket Enclosures","110.28 Enclosure Types","312.2 Damp or Wet Locations","408.37 Panelboards in Damp or Wet Locations"],"sa":2,"e":"312.2 requires cabinets and cutout boxes in damp or wet locations to be installed so water cannot enter or accumulate, and enclosures in wet locations installed on the surface of masonry to be mounted with at least 1/4 in. airspace between the enclosure and the wall. Tip: Art. 312 covers cabinets, cutout boxes and meter sockets.","r":"312.2","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What ampacity column do I use for 12 AWG NM cable with 4 current-carrying conductors?","k":["Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Cable derating","Romex","Ampacity"],"ka":0,"s":["310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","334.80 Ampacity","334.112 Insulation","334.104 Conductors"],"sa":1,"e":"334.80 states the ampacity of Type NM cable is determined using the 60 C column of Table 310.16, even if the conductors are rated 90 C, but the 90 C rating can be used for ampacity adjustment and correction as long as the final ampacity does not exceed the 60 C value. Tip: this 'derate from 90, finish at 60' rule is a classic exam question.","r":"334.80","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What clearance do I need between a recessed incandescent luminaire and wood framing?","k":["Recessed lighting","Luminaires, recessed","Clearance from combustible material","Thermal insulation"],"ka":1,"s":["410.115 Temperature","410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations","410.16 Luminaires in Clothes Closets","410.116 Clearance and Installation"],"sa":3,"e":"410.116(A) requires recessed luminaires that are not identified for contact with insulation to have at least 1/2 in. spacing from combustible material (except at points of support), and thermal insulation to be kept at least 3 in. from the enclosure, wiring compartment or ballast. Tip: IC-rated luminaires can be in contact with insulation.","r":"410.116","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"I am feeding three motors from one feeder: 10 hp, 7.5 hp and 5 hp, all 3-phase 230 volts. How do I size the feeder conductors?","k":["Several motors","Full-load current","Motors, feeder conductors","Motor circuits"],"ka":2,"s":["430.24 Several Motors or a Motor(s) and Other Load(s)","430.250 Full-Load Current, Three-Phase Alternating-Current Motors","430.22 Single Motor","430.62 Rating or Setting - Motor Feeder Short-Circuit and Ground-Fault Protection"],"sa":0,"e":"430.24 sets feeder conductor ampacity at 125 percent of the full-load current of the highest rated motor, plus the sum of the full-load currents of the other motors. From Table 430.250 at 230 volts: 10 hp is 28 A, 7.5 hp is 22 A, 5 hp is 15.2 A. 28 x 1.25 = 35 A, plus 22 plus 15.2 equals 72.2 A. Tip: use the table full-load current, not the nameplate.","r":"430.24","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the largest breaker I can use on a motor feeder that has branch-circuit breakers already?","k":["Motor circuits","Overcurrent protection","Feeders","Motors, feeder protection"],"ka":3,"s":["430.24 Several Motors or a Motor(s) and Other Load(s)","430.52 Rating or Setting for Individual Motor Circuit","215.3 Overcurrent Protection","430.62 Rating or Setting - Motor Feeder Short-Circuit and Ground-Fault Protection"],"sa":3,"e":"430.62(A) says the feeder protective device shall have a rating or setting not greater than the largest rating or setting of the branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protective device for any motor supplied by the feeder (based on the maximum device permitted by 430.52), plus the sum of the full-load currents of the other motors. Tip: Art. 430 Part V covers feeders; Part IV covers branch circuits.","r":"430.62(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"The control panel for the machinery has to be marked for its short-circuit current rating. Which section applies?","k":["Industrial control panels","Industrial machinery","Control panels","Short-circuit current rating"],"ka":0,"s":["409.110 Marking","110.10 Circuit Impedance, Short-Circuit Current Ratings, and Other Characteristics","110.24 Available Fault Current","670.3 Machine Nameplate Data"],"sa":0,"e":"409.110 requires an industrial control panel to be marked with the manufacturer's name, voltage, number of phases, supply current, short-circuit current rating, and other data. Art. 409 covers industrial control panels; Art. 670 covers the machine itself. Tip: look at the panel's own article first.","r":"409.110","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Are splices allowed in a wireway?","k":["Metal wireways","Splices","Auxiliary gutters","Wireways"],"ka":3,"s":["110.14 Electrical Connections","376.22 Number of Conductors and Ampacity","376.56 Splices, Taps, and Power Distribution Blocks","300.13 Mechanical and Electrical Continuity - Conductors"],"sa":2,"e":"376.56(A) permits splices and taps in a wireway if they are accessible and the conductors, including splices and taps, do not fill more than 75 percent of the wireway area at that point. Tip: 20 percent is for the number of conductors; 75 percent is for splice points.","r":"376.56(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What protection does a 120-volt underwater light in an inground pool need?","k":["Underwater lighting","Luminaires","Ground-fault circuit interrupters","Swimming pools"],"ka":3,"s":["680.23 Underwater Luminaires","680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment","680.26 Equipotential Bonding","410.10 Luminaires in Specific Locations"],"sa":0,"e":"680.23 covers underwater luminaires: those operating at more than 15 volts (supplied from an isolating transformer rated 15 volts or less excepted) must have GFCI protection, and wet-niche luminaires must be bonded and have a forming shell. Tip: the pool article puts luminaires in the section after receptacles.","r":"680.23","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"The solar array has a DC string that can arc. What protection does the Code require?","k":["Direct-current circuits","Arc-fault circuit interrupters","Solar","Photovoltaic systems"],"ka":3,"s":["690.11 Arc-Fault Circuit Protection (Direct Current)","690.9 Overcurrent Protection","690.12 Rapid Shutdown of PV Systems on Buildings","210.12 Arc-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection"],"sa":0,"e":"690.11 requires PV systems with dc circuits operating at 80 volts or greater between any two conductors to be protected by a listed PV arc-fault circuit interrupter or other component listed to provide equivalent protection. 210.12 is for ac branch circuits. Tip: the index lists it under Photovoltaic systems, not under AFCI in general.","r":"690.11","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"We are designing a 5 MW solar farm that sells power to the utility. Which article applies?","k":["Interconnected power production sources","Large-scale systems","Solar farms","Photovoltaic systems"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic (PV) Systems","Art. 691 Large-Scale Photovoltaic (PV) Electric Supply Stations","Art. 694 Wind Electric Systems","Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 691 applies to large-scale PV electric supply stations with a generating capacity of at least 5000 kW that are not under exclusive control of an electric utility. Smaller systems use Art. 690. Tip: both articles are listed under Photovoltaic systems, so check the size.","r":"Art. 691","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Which article covers a natural gas fuel cell installed behind a commercial building?","k":["Hazardous locations","Generators","Fuel cell systems","Interconnected power sources"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 692 Fuel Cell Systems","Art. 691 Large-Scale Photovoltaic (PV) Electric Supply Stations","Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","Art. 445 Generators"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 692 covers fuel cell power systems, including their disconnecting means, wiring and grounding. A fuel cell is not a generator in the sense of Art. 445. Tip: use the name of the equipment as the index term.","r":"Art. 692","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A farm installs a small wind turbine tied to the house. Where are the rules?","k":["Interconnected power sources","Wind electric systems","Generators","Wind turbines"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 694 Wind Electric Systems","Art. 692 Fuel Cell Systems","Art. 445 Generators","Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 694 covers wind electric systems, including the turbine, wiring, disconnects and grounding. If the system connects to the utility, Art. 705 also applies. Tip: always look for the second article that applies to the connection.","r":"Art. 694","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"The office has cubicle partitions with built-in receptacles and lights. Which article covers that wiring?","k":["Lighting","Office furnishings","Cubicle partitions","Receptacles"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 604 Manufactured Wiring Systems","Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","Art. 605 Office Furnishings"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 605 covers electrically energized office furnishings, such as lighting accessories, wired partitions, and cord-and-plug-connected furnishings. Tip: 'Office furnishings' is the index term and the title of the article.","r":"Art. 605","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"An auditorium has a public address system with amplifiers and speakers. Where is the wiring covered?","k":["Speakers","Sound systems","Assembly occupancies","Audio signal processing, amplification, and reproduction equipment"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing, Amplification, and Reproduction Equipment","Art. 520 Theaters, Audience Areas of Motion Picture and Television Studios, Performance Areas, and Similar Locations","Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 640 covers audio systems such as public address, sound reinforcement, and recording and playback equipment. The Class 2 wiring in Art. 725 may also apply to the speaker wiring. Tip: the index names for this equipment are long; scan for 'audio'.","r":"Art. 640","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A church wants an electrical rule check for a pipe organ. Is there a section?","k":["Churches","Pipe organs","Assembly occupancies","Musical instruments"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 640 Audio Signal Processing, Amplification, and Reproduction Equipment","Art. 650 Pipe Organs"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 650 covers the electrical wiring and equipment of pipe organs, including conductor sizes and overcurrent protection for the blower and control circuits. Tip: some equipment articles in Chapter 6 are only a page or two long.","r":"Art. 650","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A manufacturer is installing an industrial X-ray unit to inspect welds. Which article gives the conductor and disconnect rules?","k":["Imaging equipment","X-ray equipment","Industrial equipment","Radiation equipment"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 660 X-Ray Equipment","Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","Art. 427 Fixed Electric Heating Equipment for Pipelines and Vessels","Art. 665 Induction and Dielectric Heating Equipment"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 660 covers X-ray equipment operating at any frequency or voltage for industrial or other nonmedical or nondental use. X-ray equipment in medical and dental offices falls under Art. 517 Part V. Tip: read the scope of each article before choosing.","r":"Art. 660","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A plant uses induction heating coils to heat metal parts. Where do I find the guarding and grounding rules?","k":["Dielectric heating","Heating equipment","Industrial machinery","Induction heating"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 427 Fixed Electric Heating Equipment for Pipelines and Vessels","Art. 670 Industrial Machinery","Art. 424 Fixed Electric Space-Heating Equipment","Art. 665 Induction and Dielectric Heating Equipment"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 665 covers induction and dielectric heating equipment, including the installation and protection of the supply and control equipment. Art. 424 is for space heating in buildings. Tip: the index entry is exact.","r":"Art. 665","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A smelter uses large DC cell lines for aluminum production. Which article covers those lines?","k":["Aluminum smelting","Industrial processes","Direct-current systems","Electrolytic cells"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 668 Electrolytic Cells","Art. 669 Electroplating","Art. 665 Induction and Dielectric Heating Equipment","Art. 670 Industrial Machinery"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 668 applies to the installation of the electrical components and accessory equipment of electrolytic cells, cell lines and process power supply. Electroplating (Art. 669) is a related but separate process. Tip: the index term names the process.","r":"Art. 668","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A shop has a plating line with rectifiers. Where do I find the electrical requirements?","k":["Industrial processes","Electrolytic cells","Rectifiers","Electroplating"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 669 Electroplating","Art. 668 Electrolytic Cells","Art. 480 Storage Batteries","Art. 665 Induction and Dielectric Heating Equipment"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 669 covers the electrical circuits and equipment for electroplating, anodizing, electropolishing and electrostripping. Electrolytic cells for production of aluminum or other metals are in Art. 668. Tip: use the process name from the question.","r":"Art. 669","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A truck stop wants to provide plug-in power for parked tractor-trailers. Which article applies?","k":["Truck stops","Electric vehicle charging","Electrified truck parking spaces","Parking lots"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks","Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings, and Commercial and Noncommercial Docking Facilities","Art. 626 Electrified Truck Parking Spaces","Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 626 covers electrified truck parking spaces that provide power to refrigerated trailers, cab heating and air-conditioning, and similar loads. Art. 625 covers electric vehicle charging, which is a different equipment type. Tip: the term 'Electrified truck parking' is in the index.","r":"Art. 626","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"How is the area around a gasoline dispenser classified?","k":["Service stations","Gasoline dispensers","Hazardous (classified) locations","Motor fuel dispensing facilities"],"ka":3,"s":["511.3 Area Classification, General","515.3 Class I Locations","514.3 Classification of Locations","514.11 Circuit Disconnects"],"sa":2,"e":"514.3 and Table 514.3(B)(1) classify the dispenser interior, the area around it and the area below grade as Class I, Division 1 or 2, depending on the distance from the dispenser and the pump type. Tip: the table is the key to the extent of the classified areas.","r":"514.3(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A tank farm stores gasoline in aboveground tanks and loads trucks. Which article covers the electrical classification?","k":["Tank farms","Bulk storage plants","Hazardous (classified) locations","Flammable liquids"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 516 Spray Application, Dipping, Coating, and Printing Processes Using Flammable or Combustible Materials","Art. 513 Aircraft Hangars","Art. 515 Bulk Storage Plants","Art. 514 Motor Fuel Dispensing Facilities"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 515 covers bulk storage plants, where gasoline or other volatile flammable liquids are stored in tanks and transferred, and classifies the areas around tanks, loading racks and pumps. Art. 514 is for retail dispensing. Tip: choose by the operation, not the fuel.","r":"Art. 515","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Does a hospital need ground-fault protection for equipment at both the service and the feeder?","k":["Hospitals","Feeders","Ground-fault protection of equipment","Health care facilities"],"ka":3,"s":["215.10 Ground-Fault Protection of Equipment","517.13 Equipment Grounding Conductor for Receptacles and Fixed Electric Equipment in Patient Care Spaces","517.17 Ground-Fault Protection","230.95 Ground-Fault Protection of Equipment"],"sa":2,"e":"517.17 requires that where ground-fault protection is provided on the service or feeder disconnect by 230.95 or 215.10 in a hospital or similar building, an additional level of ground-fault protection be provided at the next level of feeder disconnects downstream, with at least six cycles of separation between the two levels. Tip: Art. 517 builds on the usual 230.95 rules.","r":"517.17","d":2,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A park model trailer will be installed in a campground. Which article covers its wiring?","k":["Park trailers","Mobile homes","Campgrounds","Recreational vehicles"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 550 Mobile Homes, Manufactured Homes, and Mobile Home Parks","Art. 552 Park Trailers","Art. 545 Manufactured Buildings","Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 552 covers park trailers, which are built on a single chassis and designed for seasonal use, not for permanent residence, and are limited in size. They are not the same as RVs (Art. 551) or mobile homes (Art. 550). Tip: use the vehicle type in the index.","r":"Art. 552","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A floating house on a lake gets its power from shore. Which article covers its wiring?","k":["Houseboats","Floating buildings","Docks","Marinas and boatyards"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 553 Floating Buildings","Art. 551 Recreational Vehicles and Recreational Vehicle Parks","Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings, and Commercial and Noncommercial Docking Facilities"],"sa":0,"e":"Art. 553 covers the electrical wiring and equipment of floating buildings, including the service, feeder and grounding arrangement from shore. Marinas (Art. 555) cover docks and boats. Tip: the more specific article is chosen first.","r":"Art. 553","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"A movie theater projection booth has film projectors and arc lamps. Where are the rules?","k":["Motion picture projectors","Projection rooms","Assembly occupancies","Theaters"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios and Similar Locations","Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","Art. 540 Motion Picture Projection Rooms","Art. 520 Theaters, Audience Areas of Motion Picture and Television Studios, Performance Areas, and Similar Locations"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 540 covers motion picture projection rooms, projectors and associated equipment, including wiring methods and ventilation. Art. 520 covers theater stages and audience areas. Tip: separate articles exist for each type of space in an entertainment venue.","r":"Art. 540","d":1,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What can serve as the power source for a power-limited fire alarm circuit?","k":["Power supplies","Power-limited circuits","Fire alarm systems","Class 3 circuits"],"ka":2,"s":["725.121 Power Sources for Class 2 and Class 3 Circuits","Chapter 9, Table 12(A) PLFA Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations","760.121 Power Sources for Power-Limited Fire Alarm Circuits","760.41 Power Sources for NPLFA Circuits"],"sa":2,"e":"760.121 requires the power source for a PLFA circuit to be a listed PLFA power source or listed Class 3 transformer, power supply or equipment marked for the PLFA power source. Tip: the NPLFA version is 760.41; the Chapter 9 tables show the limits.","r":"760.121","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A control circuit runs at 24 volts DC and 30 volts is the maximum anywhere on site. Which article covers circuits under 50 volts that are not Class 2?","k":["Control circuits","Low-voltage circuits","Circuits under 50 volts","Class 2 circuits"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","Art. 411 Lighting Systems Operating at 30 Volts or Less and Lighting Equipment Connected to Class 2 Power Sources","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 720 Circuits and Equipment Operating at Less Than 50 Volts"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 720 covers installations operating at less than 50 volts, ac or dc, other than those covered in other articles such as Class 2 circuits. It includes ampacity and wiring method rules for low-voltage systems. Tip: the title says 'Less Than 50 Volts' and the index follows it.","r":"Art. 720","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"An owner wants a building energy management system that controls loads. Which article applies?","k":["Automatic load control","Peak shaving","Load management","Energy management systems"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","Art. 750 Energy Management Systems","Art. 220 Branch-Circuit, Feeder, and Service Calculations","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 750 covers energy management systems that monitor and control loads and sources, including the effect on load calculations and overcurrent protection. Tip: use the system name from the question as the index term.","r":"Art. 750","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"We are pulling fiber optic cable through a commercial building. Where are the NEC rules for it?","k":["Optical fiber cables","Communications circuits","Fiber optics","Wiring methods"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 645 Information Technology Equipment","Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 770 covers optical fiber cables and raceways, including non-conductive and conductive types, and fire resistance and firestopping requirements. It is in Chapter 7, not Chapter 8. Tip: the index lists the cable type directly.","r":"Art. 770","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What sign must be at the service equipment where an emergency generator is installed?","k":["Signs","Service equipment","Generators","Emergency systems"],"ka":3,"s":["702.7 Signs","700.7 Signs","230.2 Number of Services","701.7 Signs"],"sa":1,"e":"700.7 requires a sign at the service-entrance equipment indicating the type and location of the on-site emergency power sources. Art. 701 and 702 have similar rules for their systems. Tip: match the article to the type of system.","r":"700.7","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"What is the internal area of a 2 in. schedule 80 PVC conduit?","k":["Rigid polyvinyl chloride conduit","PVC","Conduit fill","Raceways, dimensions"],"ka":3,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","352.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing"],"sa":3,"e":"Table 4 has a separate section for each raceway article; for Schedule 80 PVC use the Article 352 Schedule 80 section. Schedule 80 has a thicker wall than schedule 40, so the usable area is smaller. Tip: always pick the right article block in Table 4.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 4 (Article 352 Schedule 80)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the area of a 6 AWG THWN conductor for a conduit fill calculation?","k":["Conduit fill","Wire gauge","Conductors, dimensions","Conductors, size"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 5 lists dimensions for insulated conductors such as THHN and THWN; 6 AWG THHN or THWN-2 is 0.0507 sq in. Use Table 8 only for bare conductors. Tip: the insulation type is a column heading in Table 5.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 5","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Which table do I use for the ac resistance of 4/0 aluminum in steel conduit?","k":["Impedance","Voltage drop","Aluminum conductors","Conductors, alternating-current resistance and reactance"],"ka":3,"s":["Table 310.16 Allowable Ampacities of Insulated Conductors Rated Up to and Including 2000 Volts","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C"],"sa":3,"e":"Table 9 has separate columns for copper and aluminum conductors in PVC, aluminum and steel conduit. Choose the aluminum conductor column and the steel conduit column. Tip: Table 8 gives only dc resistance.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 9","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"A 12-volt dc Class 2 source feeds a lighting circuit. What are its power limits?","k":["Direct-current circuits","Power source limitations","Class 2 circuits","Low-voltage lighting"],"ka":2,"s":["725.121 Power Sources for Class 2 and Class 3 Circuits","Chapter 9, Table 11(B) Class 2 and Class 3 Direct-Current Power Source Limitations","Chapter 9, Table 11(A) Class 2 and Class 3 Alternating-Current Power Source Limitations","Chapter 9, Table 12(B) PLFA Direct-Current Power Source Limitations"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 11(B) gives the dc power source limits for Class 2 and Class 3 circuits, such as the maximum voltage, current and VA for each voltage range. Use Table 11(A) for ac sources. Tip: the (A)/(B) split is always ac/dc.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 11(B)","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What internal area does a 1 1/4 in. intermediate metal conduit have for a 40 percent fill?","k":["Conduit fill","IMC","Raceways, dimensions","Intermediate metal conduit"],"ka":2,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","342.22 Number of Conductors"],"sa":0,"e":"Table 4 includes a block for Article 342 (IMC) with total area and the 40 percent column for each trade size. Choose the article for your raceway type. Tip: Article 342 is IMC.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 4 (Article 342)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the inside area of a 1 in. liquidtight flexible metal conduit for fill calculations?","k":["Raceways, dimensions","Conduit fill","Liquidtight flexible metal conduit","Flexible conduit"],"ka":0,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","350.22 Number of Conductors","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 4 has a block for Article 350 (LFMC). Use the 40 percent column for three or more conductors. Tip: see the article name in the table heading to avoid reading the wrong row.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 4 (Article 350)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"Does a receptacle on a commercial building's rooftop for HVAC service need GFCI protection?","k":["Heating, air-conditioning, and refrigeration equipment outlet","Rooftops","Receptacles","Ground-fault circuit interrupters"],"ka":3,"s":["210.8 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter Protection for Personnel","590.6 Ground-Fault Circuit-Interrupter (GFCI) Protection for Personnel","406.9 Receptacles in Damp or Wet Locations","210.63 Heating, Air-Conditioning, and Refrigeration Equipment Outlet"],"sa":0,"e":"210.8(B) covers locations other than dwelling units, and includes rooftops among the places where 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles must have GFCI protection for personnel. 210.63 tells you the HVAC outlet must be provided, while 210.8 tells you how to protect it. Tip: dwelling rules are in 210.8(A) and other-than-dwelling in 210.8(B).","r":"210.8(B)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Can I use the underground gas piping at a house as a grounding electrode?","k":["Metal underground piping","Bonding","Grounding electrodes","Gas piping"],"ka":2,"s":["250.50 Grounding Electrode System","250.104 Bonding of Piping Systems and Exposed Structural Metal","250.52 Grounding Electrodes","250.53 Grounding Electrode System Installation"],"sa":2,"e":"250.52(B)(1) says a metal underground gas piping system is not permitted to be used as a grounding electrode. The gas pipe still has to be bonded under 250.104(B). Tip: 250.52(A) lists what you can use and 250.52(B) lists what you cannot.","r":"250.52(B)(1)","d":2,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the smallest single conductor I can install in a cable tray?","k":["Single conductors","Wiring methods","Conductor sizes","Cable trays"],"ka":3,"s":["392.22 Number of Conductors or Cables","392.20 Cable and Conductor Installation","300.17 Number and Size of Conductors in Raceway","392.10 Uses Permitted"],"sa":3,"e":"392.10(B)(1) allows single conductors in cable tray in industrial establishments with qualified persons servicing, and the single conductors must be 1/0 AWG or larger. Multiconductor cables have other rules. Tip: the permitted-use section is the first stop for any article on a wiring method.","r":"392.10(B)(1)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"A 15-amp window air conditioner is plugged into a 20-amp circuit that also feeds a few other receptacles. Is that OK?","k":["Cord-connected equipment","Air-conditioning","Branch circuits","Air conditioners, room"],"ka":3,"s":["440.32 Single Motor-Compressor","440.62 Branch-Circuit Requirements","440.14 Location","210.23 Permissible Loads, Other Than Individual Branch Circuits"],"sa":1,"e":"440.62(C) says where lighting outlets, other appliances or general-use receptacle outlets are also supplied, the total marked rating of the room air conditioner shall not exceed 50 percent of the branch-circuit rating. On a 20 A circuit that is 10 A, so a 15 A unit is not allowed. 440.62(B) permits 80 percent if it is the only load. Tip: room air conditioners have a Part VII in Art. 440.","r":"440.62(C)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"Do I have to follow the instructions that came with a listed panelboard?","k":["Listing","Manufacturer instructions","Product certification","Listed equipment"],"ka":3,"s":["90.7 Examination of Equipment for Safety","110.3 Examination, Identification, Installation, Use, and Listing (Product Certification) of Equipment","110.12 Mechanical Execution of Work","110.2 Approval"],"sa":1,"e":"110.3(B) requires listed or labeled equipment to be installed and used in accordance with any instructions included in the listing or labeling. That makes the instructions part of the Code requirements. Tip: this is one of the most used sections in Chapter 1.","r":"110.3(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"I'm using a white wire in a 14-2 cable as the switched hot in a switch loop. What must I do to the white wire?","k":["White conductors","Switch loops","Conductor color code","Grounded conductors, identification"],"ka":3,"s":["210.5 Identification for Branch Circuits","200.6 Means of Identifying Grounded Conductors","200.7 Use of Insulation of a White or Gray Color or with Three Continuous White or Gray Stripes","404.2 Switch Connections"],"sa":2,"e":"200.7(C)(1) lets a white or gray conductor in a cable be used as an ungrounded supply conductor to a switch but not as a return, provided it is permanently re-identified (by tape, paint or other effective means) at each location where the conductor is visible and accessible. Tip: 200.6 tells how to identify grounded conductors, and 200.7 tells when white is used for something else.","r":"200.7(C)(1)","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the full-load current of a 2-hp, 230-volt single-phase motor for sizing the branch-circuit conductors?","k":["Horsepower ratings","Nameplate current","Motors, full-load current","Single-phase motors"],"ka":2,"s":["430.250 Full-Load Current, Three-Phase Alternating-Current Motors","430.248 Full-Load Currents in Amperes, Single-Phase Alternating-Current Motors","430.22 Single Motor","430.6 Ampacity and Motor Rating Determination"],"sa":1,"e":"Table 430.248 lists the full-load current by horsepower and voltage; for 2 hp at 230 V it is 12 A. Conductors are then sized at 125 percent (15 A). Tip: three-phase motors use Table 430.250, so always check the phase first.","r":"Table 430.248","d":3,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What is the full-load current of a 25-hp, 460-volt, 3-phase motor?","k":["Motors, full-load current","Three-phase motors","Horsepower ratings","Nameplate current"],"ka":0,"s":["430.6 Ampacity and Motor Rating Determination","430.248 Full-Load Currents in Amperes, Single-Phase Alternating-Current Motors","430.250 Full-Load Current, Three-Phase Alternating-Current Motors","430.32 Continuous-Duty Motors"],"sa":2,"e":"Table 430.250 gives 34 A for a 25-hp, 460-volt induction motor. This table value, not the nameplate, is used for sizing the branch-circuit conductors and short-circuit protection under 430.6(A)(1). Tip: nameplate current is used only for overload protection.","r":"Table 430.250","d":1,"c":"Chapters 1-4"},{"q":"What Class I group does gasoline fall in, and where is the grouping explained?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Material groups","Gasoline","Explosion-proof equipment"],"ka":0,"s":["500.5 Classifications of Locations","501.1 Scope","501.105 Meters, Instruments, and Relays","500.6 Material Groups"],"sa":3,"e":"500.6(A) lists the Class I groups: Group A acetylene, Group B hydrogen, Group C ethylene, and Group D includes gasoline, propane, acetone and similar. Equipment must be listed for the group of the hazardous material. Tip: use the 'Material groups' subentry under Hazardous (classified) locations.","r":"500.6(A)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What group does grain dust belong to for Class II locations?","k":["Hazardous (classified) locations","Material groups","Grain elevators","Combustible dusts"],"ka":0,"s":["502.1 Scope","506.5 Classification of Locations","500.5 Classifications of Locations","500.6 Material Groups"],"sa":3,"e":"500.6(B) lists the Class II groups: Group E metal dust, Group F carbonaceous dust such as coal, and Group G grain, wood, plastic and chemical dusts. Tip: you need the group to select equipment under Art. 502.","r":"500.6(B)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"What makes a location Class I, Division 2 instead of Division 1?","k":["Explosion-proof equipment","Division 2","Hazardous (classified) locations","Flammable gases"],"ka":2,"s":["500.7 Protection Techniques","500.5 Classifications of Locations","500.6 Material Groups","501.10 Wiring Methods"],"sa":1,"e":"500.5(B)(2) describes Class I, Division 2: locations where volatile flammable liquids or gases are handled, processed or used but are normally confined within closed containers or systems, or where ignitable concentrations are prevented by positive mechanical ventilation, or locations adjacent to Division 1. Tip: Division 1 means present under normal conditions; Division 2 means only under abnormal ones.","r":"500.5(B)(2)","d":3,"c":"Chapter 5"},{"q":"Does each arc welder need its own disconnect, and where is that rule?","k":["Welders, electric","Welding machines","Arc welders","Disconnecting means"],"ka":0,"s":["630.11 Ampacity of Supply Conductors","430.102 Location","630.13 Disconnecting Means","630.12 Overcurrent Protection"],"sa":2,"e":"630.13 requires a disconnecting means in the supply circuit for each arc welder that does not have one mounted as an integral part of the welder, and the rating must be sufficient to accommodate the overcurrent protection specified in 630.12. Tip: the sections for welders follow supply conductors, overcurrent protection, then disconnect.","r":"630.13","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Where is the emergency power-off requirement for a computer room found?","k":["Information technology equipment","Disconnecting means","Emergency shutoff","Data centers"],"ka":0,"s":["110.22 Identification of Disconnecting Means","645.10 Disconnecting Means","645.4 Special Requirements for Information Technology Equipment Room","700.12 General Requirements"],"sa":1,"e":"645.10 requires a means to disconnect power to all electronic equipment in the information technology equipment room, operable from a readily accessible location at principal exit doors, and to disconnect HVAC systems serving the room. Tip: Art. 645 is the optional article for IT rooms and has its own disconnect section.","r":"645.10","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"The contractor wants to use factory-assembled cable assemblies with connectors to wire a suspended ceiling. Which article applies?","k":["Cable assemblies","Manufactured wiring systems","Prefabricated wiring","Ceilings"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","Art. 330 Metal-Clad Cable: Type MC","Art. 605 Office Furnishings","Art. 604 Manufactured Wiring Systems"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 604 covers field-installed manufactured wiring systems with factory-assembled cable and connectors, used in accessible and concealed spaces. Art. 330 covers MC cable itself. Tip: 'Manufactured wiring systems' is the index term.","r":"Art. 604","d":2,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"Lighting and a pump are going in at a pond and irrigation canal on a farm. Which article covers bodies of water that are not pools or marinas?","k":["Canals","Ponds","Lakes","Natural and artificially made bodies of water"],"ka":3,"s":["Art. 680 Swimming Pools, Fountains, and Similar Installations","Art. 675 Electrically Driven or Controlled Irrigation Machines","Art. 682 Natural and Artificially Made Bodies of Water","Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings, and Commercial and Noncommercial Docking Facilities"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 682 covers the installation of electrical wiring and equipment in, on, or adjacent to natural or artificially made bodies of water not covered by other articles, such as lakes, canals, ponds and quarries. Tip: if it is not a pool, fountain, or marina, check Art. 682.","r":"Art. 682","d":1,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"A homeowner wants a storable above-ground pool with a cord-connected filter pump. Which section covers that pump?","k":["Storable pools","Cord-and-plug-connected equipment","Pumps","Swimming pools"],"ka":3,"s":["680.25 Feeders","680.26 Equipotential Bonding","680.31 Pumps","680.22 Area Lighting, Receptacles, and Equipment"],"sa":2,"e":"680.31 covers pumps for storable pools: a cord-and-plug-connected pump must incorporate an approved system of double insulation and a means for grounding, and must be supplied by a GFCI-protected circuit. Storable pools have their own Part III in Art. 680. Tip: permanently installed pools are Part II, storable pools are Part III.","r":"680.31","d":3,"c":"Chapter 6"},{"q":"An internet provider installs fiber to the house with an optical network terminal powered from inside the home. Which article covers that?","k":["Fiber to the home","Premises-powered broadband communications systems","Broadband","Optical network terminals"],"ka":1,"s":["Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","Art. 770 Optical Fiber Cables and Raceways","Art. 830 Network-Powered Broadband Communications Systems","Art. 840 Premises-Powered Broadband Communications Systems"],"sa":3,"e":"Art. 840 covers premises-powered broadband systems, such as fiber to the premises where the terminal equipment is powered from the customer's wiring. Network-powered systems (Art. 830) get their power from the network. Tip: where the power comes from decides the article.","r":"Art. 840","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A cable operator runs coax with power from a network power supply to a broadband interface device on the side of the house. Which article applies?","k":["Network interface units","Coaxial cable","Network-powered broadband communications systems","Broadband"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 830 Network-Powered Broadband Communications Systems","Art. 840 Premises-Powered Broadband Communications Systems","Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 830 covers network-powered broadband communications systems, in which the network provides the power to the interface device. Tip: compare the sources of power: network (Art. 830) versus premises (Art. 840).","r":"Art. 830","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A hospital stair pressurization fan needs wiring that keeps working in a fire for two hours. Which article covers the cable system?","k":["Fire-resistive cable systems","Emergency systems","Fire pumps","Circuit integrity cable"],"ka":0,"s":["Art. 700 Emergency Systems","Art. 728 Fire-Resistive Cable Systems","Art. 760 Fire Alarm Systems","Art. 695 Fire Pumps"],"sa":1,"e":"Art. 728 covers fire-resistive cable systems, which are cables listed to maintain circuit integrity for a specified time (such as 2 hours) in a fire. Art. 700 specifies where emergency circuits require this type of protection. Tip: look for the specific cable-system article that fits the product.","r":"Art. 728","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"A plant runs Type ITC instrumentation tray cable in cable trays for process controls. Which article is for that?","k":["Cable trays","Type ITC","Instrumentation tray cable","Process control wiring"],"ka":2,"s":["Art. 336 Power and Control Tray Cable: Type TC","Art. 725 Class 1, Class 2, and Class 3 Remote-Control, Signaling, and Power-Limited Circuits","Art. 727 Instrumentation Tray Cable: Type ITC","Art. 392 Cable Trays"],"sa":2,"e":"Art. 727 covers the use, installation and construction specifications of instrumentation tray cable, Type ITC. It applies to circuits limited to 150 volts and 5 amperes. Tip: Art. 392 covers the tray itself.","r":"Art. 727","d":1,"c":"Chapters 7-8"},{"q":"How many 14 AWG THHN conductors fit in a 1/2 in. EMT?","k":["Raceways, number of conductors in","Conductors, size","Electrical metallic tubing","Conduit and tubing fill tables"],"ka":3,"s":["Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","Chapter 9, Table 1 Percent of Cross Section of Conduit and Tubing for Conductors and Cables","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","358.22 Number of Conductors"],"sa":0,"e":"Annex C, Table C.1 shows 12 conductors for 14 AWG THHN in a 1/2 in. EMT. The check: 0.122 sq in. (40 percent) divided by 0.0097 sq in. gives 12.6, which rounds down to 12. Tip: round down; you cannot have a fraction of a wire.","r":"Annex C, Table C.1","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"How many 6 AWG THHN conductors can go in a 1 in. EMT?","k":["Conductor fill","Raceways, number of conductors in","Electrical metallic tubing","Conduit and tubing fill tables"],"ka":3,"s":["Chapter 9, Table 9 Alternating-Current Resistance and Reactance for 600-Volt Cables, 3-Phase, 60 Hz, 75 C","Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","358.22 Number of Conductors","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing"],"sa":1,"e":"Table C.1 gives 6 conductors of 6 AWG THHN for a 1 in. EMT; check 0.346 sq in. divided by 0.0507 sq in. gives 6.8, so 6. Tip: if the wires are all the same size and type, the annex gives the answer quickly.","r":"Annex C, Table C.1","d":1,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"},{"q":"What is the cross-sectional area of a 4/0 THHN conductor?","k":["Conductor area","Conductors, dimensions","Conductors, insulated","Circular mils"],"ka":1,"s":["Annex C Conduit and Tubing Fill Tables for Conductors and Fixture Wires of the Same Size","Chapter 9, Table 8 Conductor Properties","Chapter 9, Table 4 Dimensions and Percent Area of Conduit and Tubing","Chapter 9, Table 5 Dimensions of Insulated Conductors and Fixture Wires"],"sa":3,"e":"Table 5 lists 4/0 AWG THHN at 0.3237 sq in. The Table 8 area (211,600 cmil) is the copper only. Tip: Table 5 columns are for the insulation type you are using.","r":"Chapter 9, Table 5","d":2,"c":"Chapter 9 and annexes"}];